WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.128
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4546.

2
00:00:01.128 --> 00:00:02.257
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.257 --> 00:00:02.633
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:04.514
Today is November eleventh, 2015.

5
00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:05.642
It's a Wednesday.

6
00:00:05.642 --> 00:00:07.147
It's actually Veteran's Day.

7
00:00:07.147 --> 00:00:14.294
I did not know that until I tried to go to the library this morning and it was closed.

8
00:00:14.294 --> 00:00:18.807
I'm Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History,

9
00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:27.459
and I'm here today with Ms. Marie Towner to talk about her memories growing up and everything that she knows about Mossville [Louisiana].

10
00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:33.101
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

11
00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:41.000
So I just want to thank you for taking the time with us today, Ms. Marie, to share your stories.

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.086
<v MARIE TOWNER>Oh, okay.

13
00:00:42.086 --> 00:00:52.400
<v ARSENEAULT>So for the record, could you state . . . or just for the recording, really, could you state your full name?

14
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.029
<v TOWNER>Marie Perkins Towner.

15
00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:54.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

16
00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:58.371
And when and where were you born?

17
00:00:58.371 --> 00:01:00.000
<v TOWNER>Mossville . . . eleven-fourteen-thirty-eight.

18
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.970
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you born in your home?

19
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:04.632
<v TOWNER>Yes.

20
00:01:04.632 --> 00:01:08.602
Yes, I was born at home.

21
00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:12.571
Damn, I hadn't thought about that.

22
00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:17.865
I mean, darn I hadn't thought about that.

23
00:01:17.865 --> 00:01:18.526
Yeah.

24
00:01:18.526 --> 00:01:24.481
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom ever tell you your birth story?

25
00:01:24.481 --> 00:01:28.451
<v TOWNER>No. Just the last two kids.

26
00:01:28.451 --> 00:01:31.759
I think she had . . . well,

27
00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:43.669
but she went to the hospital with those two because they were . . . she had to have a C-section.

28
00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:54.256
But as far as I know, it was no complications with me and my oldest brother.

29
00:01:54.256 --> 00:02:02.195
And we lived near my grandmother, which I don't remember living there.

30
00:02:02.195 --> 00:02:08.150
I only remember living on the Old Spanish Trail.

31
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:16.090
And then in Queensboro [suburb of Mossville], I believe, it was called.

32
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.075
Yes, in Queensboro.

33
00:02:18.075 --> 00:02:22.707
My parents bought a home down there.

34
00:02:22.707 --> 00:02:28.000
Then I went to California in '61.

35
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.689
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your parents' names?

36
00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:34.641
<v TOWNER>[Bernell?] and Grace Perkins.

37
00:02:34.641 --> 00:02:40.544
My mother was a Moss before she married.

38
00:02:40.544 --> 00:02:48.660
And my father's . . . I don't know . . . I didn't know his father.

39
00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.825
I don't think he even knew him.

40
00:02:53.825 --> 00:02:58.252
But his mother was Minnie Miller.

41
00:02:58.252 --> 00:03:04.893
And my mother's parents were Beatrice and Oliver Moss.

42
00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:18.913
I don't really . . . I really don't know that much about them, except my grandfather did a lot of farming.

43
00:03:18.913 --> 00:03:21.126
<v ARSENEAULT>Your grandfather Moss?

44
00:03:21.126 --> 00:03:22.602
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes.

45
00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:32.932
He had horses and cows and chickens and all of that kind of stuff.

46
00:03:32.932 --> 00:03:44.000
But like I said, I married really young and cut out and left [laughs].

47
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.586
<v ARSENEAULT>What did your parents do for a living?

48
00:03:49.586 --> 00:03:54.473
<v TOWNER>My dad worked at a refinery, Conoco.

49
00:03:54.473 --> 00:04:02.851
For as long as I can remember he was working at Conoco.

50
00:04:02.851 --> 00:04:05.644
My mom didn't work.

51
00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:09.833
Yes, she didn't work at all.

52
00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:16.117
Oh, she ran a grocery store for a guy.

53
00:04:16.117 --> 00:04:21.703
Darn, I can't think of his name now.

54
00:04:21.703 --> 00:04:32.176
But that's about the only job she ever had, and that didn't last too long.

55
00:04:32.176 --> 00:04:43.347
Yes, but I guess it's kind of strange seeing Mossville no homes or nothing like that.

56
00:04:43.347 --> 00:04:52.423
But then they started buying up in Westlake [Louisiana] by the railroad track.

57
00:04:52.423 --> 00:05:00.104
They had people who living all up and down the highway,

58
00:05:00.104 --> 00:05:14.766
and they all sold out and most of the people moved either to Lake Charles [Louisiana] or out of town completely.

59
00:05:14.766 --> 00:05:24.541
I'm trying to think of the teacher's name at the school I went to.

60
00:05:24.541 --> 00:05:32.221
See, I didn't go to . . . The high school opened in '56.

61
00:05:32.221 --> 00:05:37.806
I was already married, had a couple kids.

62
00:05:37.806 --> 00:05:46.185
So I went to Mossville School, but it was just two rooms.

63
00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:53.865
And then they added another room, so that made it three.

64
00:05:53.865 --> 00:05:54.563
Wow.

65
00:05:54.563 --> 00:06:02.243
They had three teachers: Ms. Washington, Ms. Phillips, and Ms. Mitchell.

66
00:06:02.243 --> 00:06:19.000
They were the only three teachers that I ever had, besides after I got grown and went to . . . back and got my diploma.

67
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:27.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad ever talk about what he did at the plant?

68
00:06:27.571 --> 00:06:29.714
<v TOWNER>No, he didn't.

69
00:06:29.714 --> 00:06:34.000
I don't know what he did.

70
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:46.143
Couldn't have been too much of nothing, because he wasn't no big time hard working man [laughs].

71
00:06:46.143 --> 00:06:50.429
I don't know what he did.

72
00:06:50.429 --> 00:06:52.571
I have no

73
00:06:52.571 --> 00:06:53.286
<c.p>idea.

74
00:06:53.286 --> 00:06:54.000
None.

75
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:03.286
Oh, and he worked for Johnson’s Funeral Home after he retired from Conoco.

76
00:07:03.286 --> 00:07:14.714
Yes, and he did some of everything there because this is when Johnson's first started out.

77
00:07:14.714 --> 00:07:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the funeral home?

78
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:30.047
<v TOWNER>I'm thinking it may be in the same place it is now where it was then, on Lake Street.

79
00:07:30.047 --> 00:07:39.930
I haven't been by . . . Oh, I haven't seen Mr. Zeb in years, actually since my father passed.

80
00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:46.326
But he worked for him . . . well, when he first started out.

81
00:07:46.326 --> 00:07:56.791
Oh, and then they moved to California for a couple of years, and then they came back here.

82
00:07:56.791 --> 00:08:02.023
That's when Daddy went to work for Mr. Zeb.

83
00:08:02.023 --> 00:08:02.605
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

84
00:08:02.605 --> 00:08:17.721
<v TOWNER>And like I said, I don't know how long he worked for him, but I'm guessing until they moved to California for a couple of years.

85
00:08:17.721 --> 00:08:25.860
And after they came and moved back here, I don't think he did anything.

86
00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.930
He was getting a little old then.

87
00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And your mom was just a . . .

88
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:34.681
<v TOWNER>Housewife.

89
00:08:34.681 --> 00:08:40.128
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things was she responsible for?

90
00:08:40.128 --> 00:08:41.489
<v TOWNER>Nothing [laughs].

91
00:08:41.489 --> 00:08:42.851
She wasn't.

92
00:08:42.851 --> 00:08:48.979
She would . . . Just the kids, and that was it.

93
00:08:48.979 --> 00:08:51.021
Just the kids.

94
00:08:51.021 --> 00:08:53.064
<v ARSENEAULT>How many kids?

95
00:08:53.064 --> 00:08:53.745
<v TOWNER>Four.

96
00:08:53.745 --> 00:08:57.149
It was four of us.

97
00:08:57.149 --> 00:09:06.000
Our baby sister passed back in '86, I'm thinking . . . somewhere like that.

98
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.383
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the order?

99
00:09:08.383 --> 00:09:08.979
<v TOWNER>Huh?

100
00:09:08.979 --> 00:09:13.745
<v ARSENEAULT>The order of the siblings and their names.

101
00:09:13.745 --> 00:09:16.128
<v TOWNER>I am the oldest.

102
00:09:16.128 --> 00:09:21.489
Then there's Walter Perkins, Robert Perkins, and Patsy Perkins.

103
00:09:21.489 --> 00:09:23.872
And that was it.

104
00:09:23.872 --> 00:09:34.000
As far as I know, Daddy didn't have any more kids and Mother definitely didn't have any.

105
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.766
So it was just the four of us.

106
00:09:38.766 --> 00:09:46.511
And my youngest sister passed away, I think, in '86 . . . something like that.

107
00:09:46.511 --> 00:09:50.085
Yes, and we stayed a while.

108
00:09:50.085 --> 00:09:54.851
We'd go down and visit Grandmother and Great-Grandmother.

109
00:09:54.851 --> 00:09:59.021
I didn't care for her too much.

110
00:09:59.021 --> 00:10:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Which one was that?

111
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:02.776
<v TOWNER>The great-grandmother.

112
00:10:02.776 --> 00:10:04.327
She was mean [laughs].

113
00:10:04.327 --> 00:10:05.878
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she a Towner?

114
00:10:05.878 --> 00:10:07.816
Or was she a Perkins?

115
00:10:07.816 --> 00:10:09.367
<v TOWNER>Oh, no. Neither one.

116
00:10:09.367 --> 00:10:10.918
She was a Williams.

117
00:10:10.918 --> 00:10:11.694
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

118
00:10:11.694 --> 00:10:14.408
<v TOWNER>Let's see what . . . the heck was she?

119
00:10:14.408 --> 00:10:17.510
I got a picture of them back there.

120
00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:19.449
I can't remember my grandfather.

121
00:10:19.449 --> 00:10:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She was grouchy?

122
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.985
<v TOWNER>Oh, was she!

123
00:10:22.985 --> 00:10:26.954
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about her?

124
00:10:26.954 --> 00:10:30.262
<v TOWNER>That she was mean [laughs].

125
00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:34.892
But she was a . . . just the times.

126
00:10:34.892 --> 00:10:40.846
I guess she was just like every other great-grandmother.

127
00:10:40.846 --> 00:10:44.815
They'd whip you in a minute.

128
00:10:44.815 --> 00:10:46.800
And she did.

129
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:52.754
I guess that's the reason I didn't like her.

130
00:10:52.754 --> 00:11:00.692
But my grandmother . . . Neither one of my grandmothers whipped or spanked us.

131
00:11:00.692 --> 00:11:05.985
And neither one of our grandfathers, Father either.

132
00:11:05.985 --> 00:11:12.600
So we had a pretty good life during those days.

133
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:20.538
It wasn't bad at all, because like I said, Daddy was working.

134
00:11:20.538 --> 00:11:25.169
So life was pretty good for us.

135
00:11:25.169 --> 00:11:31.123
I think we had the first telephone in Mossville.

136
00:11:31.123 --> 00:11:34.431
And we had a TV.

137
00:11:34.431 --> 00:11:37.738
We'd have company every night.

138
00:11:37.738 --> 00:11:38.400
Yeah.

139
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.354
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, they were coming over to watch the TV?

140
00:11:44.354 --> 00:11:47.000
<v TOWNER>Watch TV, [Agrees].

141
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.336
<v ARSENEAULT>What would y'all watch?

142
00:11:49.336 --> 00:11:53.425
<v TOWNER>Oh, I don't know . . . in the '50s.

143
00:11:53.425 --> 00:12:05.106
Whatever it was, it went off early, so we didn't have to be bothered with them half of the night.

144
00:12:05.106 --> 00:12:08.611
But yes, it went off early.

145
00:12:08.611 --> 00:12:13.867
Oh gosh, what was the name of that show?

146
00:12:13.867 --> 00:12:20.292
The Texaco program with Milton Berle, that was a weekly show.

147
00:12:20.292 --> 00:12:22.044
And what else?

148
00:12:22.044 --> 00:12:28.469
I don't know what . . . Bringing up Father or Father Knows Best.

149
00:12:28.469 --> 00:12:30.805
And Red Skeleton Show.

150
00:12:30.805 --> 00:12:32.558
Shows like that.

151
00:12:32.558 --> 00:12:35.478
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Taking pictures?

152
00:12:35.478 --> 00:12:36.646
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Yes, sir.

153
00:12:36.646 --> 00:12:42.487
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: You might get dizzy walking in there.

154
00:12:42.487 --> 00:12:45.991
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, I just came from there.

155
00:12:45.991 --> 00:12:53.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: You might get dizzy walking up in there.

156
00:12:53.000 --> 00:13:00.188
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what your grandparents did for a living?

157
00:13:00.188 --> 00:13:05.219
<v TOWNER>Okay, my Daddy's mother was a cook.

158
00:13:05.219 --> 00:13:08.094
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

159
00:13:08.094 --> 00:13:12.406
<v TOWNER>Minnie Miller . . . well, Minnie Williams Miller.

160
00:13:12.406 --> 00:13:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did she cook?

161
00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.667
<v TOWNER>It was a place called Gable Lodge.

162
00:13:19.667 --> 00:13:21.762
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that in Mossville?

163
00:13:21.762 --> 00:13:24.905
<v TOWNER>No, it was on [Highway] 90.

164
00:13:24.905 --> 00:13:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Near Lake Charles?

165
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:33.000
<v TOWNER>No. It was between Lake Charles and Sulphur [Louisiana].

166
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:33.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

167
00:13:33.667 --> 00:13:34.333
<v TOWNER>Yes.

168
00:13:34.333 --> 00:13:41.667
And it was . . . The name of it was the Gable Lodge.

169
00:13:41.667 --> 00:13:50.333
And my grandmother . . . my other grandmother, my mother's mother, was a maid there.

170
00:13:50.333 --> 00:14:03.000
And like I said, my grandfather . . . her husband was a farmer. [Mamie?] didn't have no husband, I don't think.

171
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She supported herself?

172
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.333
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, as a cook.

173
00:14:08.333 --> 00:14:17.000
She worked other places, but I remembered her working at the Gable Lodge.

174
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.000
That's about all I know.

175
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.750
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would your grandfather grow?

176
00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:26.938
<v TOWNER>Oh, beets.

177
00:14:26.938 --> 00:14:32.875
I remember him growing beets, mustard greens, cabbage, and watermelons.

178
00:14:32.875 --> 00:14:42.375
Back then I don't think they knew anything about zucchini or broccoli and nothing like that.

179
00:14:42.375 --> 00:14:46.531
I mean, he had a big area.

180
00:14:46.531 --> 00:14:48.906
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he live?

181
00:14:48.906 --> 00:14:50.094
<v TOWNER>In Mossville.

182
00:14:50.094 --> 00:14:54.250
Let's see, now what is it near?

183
00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:57.219
Oh, it's near the church.

184
00:14:57.219 --> 00:14:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Mount Zion?

185
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.138
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Mount Zion.

186
00:15:01.138 --> 00:15:08.264
They owned . . . well their family owned where Mount Zion is.

187
00:15:08.264 --> 00:15:15.391
The Mosses on my grandfather's side owned most of Mossville.

188
00:15:15.391 --> 00:15:21.805
And my dad, they were from a different area.

189
00:15:21.805 --> 00:15:26.080
They didn't really live in Mossville.

190
00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:31.782
They lived between 90 and the railroad tracks.

191
00:15:31.782 --> 00:15:34.632
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that more Westlake?

192
00:15:34.632 --> 00:15:37.483
<v TOWNER>I think so, yes.

193
00:15:37.483 --> 00:15:42.471
I think it would be more Westlake.

194
00:15:42.471 --> 00:15:51.736
It's Bayou d'Inde now, over in that area where Dunham and Price is.

195
00:15:51.736 --> 00:15:57.437
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Hey, where'd your friend go?

196
00:15:57.437 --> 00:16:01.000
Where'd your friend go?

197
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:05.098
<v ARSENEAULT>I think she's outside.

198
00:16:05.098 --> 00:16:09.195
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Oh.

199
00:16:09.195 --> 00:16:16.366
<v TOWNER>But that's the area they lived in.

200
00:16:16.366 --> 00:16:24.561
<v ARSENEAULT>How did your mom and your dad meet?

201
00:16:24.561 --> 00:16:32.756
<v TOWNER>How did they meet? [laughs] I don't know.

202
00:16:32.756 --> 00:16:37.878
I really . . . I don't know.

203
00:16:37.878 --> 00:16:43.000
I have no idea.

204
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.167
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't talk about it?

205
00:16:45.167 --> 00:16:45.600
<v TOWNER>No.

206
00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:49.067
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess that was private information back then.

207
00:16:49.067 --> 00:16:50.367
<v TOWNER>I guess so.

208
00:16:50.367 --> 00:16:53.400
There wasn't too many places to meet.

209
00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe at church or something.

210
00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:03.760
<v TOWNER>No. I don't think my dad went to church then.

211
00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:14.624
I don't think . . . He wasn't no big time church worker when he was young.

212
00:17:14.624 --> 00:17:19.280
As he got older, he was.

213
00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:20.832
Oh, wow.

214
00:17:20.832 --> 00:17:23.936
I have no idea.

215
00:17:23.936 --> 00:17:27.040
I mean, absolutely none.

216
00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:33.248
I'll have to remember to ask somebody that.

217
00:17:33.248 --> 00:17:41.008
Robert . . . I'll have to ask my brother do he know.

218
00:17:41.008 --> 00:17:44.112
But I really don't.

219
00:17:44.112 --> 00:17:47.216
They were great parents.

220
00:17:47.216 --> 00:17:51.872
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was responsible for disciplining y'all?

221
00:17:51.872 --> 00:17:53.424
<v TOWNER>Our mother.

222
00:17:53.424 --> 00:18:04.288
Our daddy never spanked us, whipped us or nothing. [laughs] No, he never did.

223
00:18:04.288 --> 00:18:10.496
It was our mother, and grandmothers and great-grandmother.

224
00:18:10.496 --> 00:18:13.600
But that was it.

225
00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:18.256
We weren't too bad growing up.

226
00:18:18.256 --> 00:18:21.360
But that's about it.

227
00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So your grandfather, did he ever sell any of his vegetables or his fruit?

228
00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:37.121
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, he sold most of them.

229
00:18:37.121 --> 00:18:43.009
And he hauled the water in a barrel . . . in barrels.

230
00:18:43.009 --> 00:18:44.776
<v ARSENEAULT>From a well?

231
00:18:44.776 --> 00:18:51.252
<v TOWNER>No. It was from a hole, a very deep, deep hole.

232
00:18:51.252 --> 00:18:55.374
They had several of them back there.

233
00:18:55.374 --> 00:19:03.028
It was off of Coach Williams Road is now, over in that area.

234
00:19:03.028 --> 00:19:05.383
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it private property?

235
00:19:05.383 --> 00:19:15.981
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees] I don't know . . . No. I think it belonged to . . . at that time it belonged to the railroad.

236
00:19:15.981 --> 00:19:21.869
It wasn't . . . As far as I know it wasn't private.

237
00:19:21.869 --> 00:19:30.112
Well I guess they would be private property if it belonged to the railroad.

238
00:19:30.112 --> 00:19:36.000
But I think that's who those holes belonged to.

239
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.567
<v ARSENEAULT>So they were kind of like community?

240
00:19:38.567 --> 00:19:38.933
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

241
00:19:38.933 --> 00:19:39.300
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

242
00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:41.133
That's what I was curious . . .

243
00:19:41.133 --> 00:19:45.167
<v TOWNER>When we would take the clothes down there and wash them . . .

244
00:19:45.167 --> 00:19:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

245
00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.280
<v TOWNER>Dynamite hole.

246
00:19:48.280 --> 00:20:03.000
They'd take their tubs down there, rub boards, and brought soap and washed, and then hang them out to dry and all that.

247
00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:15.160
And then the ones that didn't want to go down there, my grandfather would haul a barrel of water.

248
00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:24.120
I think it was about fifteen cents then [laughs] for a barrel of water.

249
00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:26.680
<v ARSENEAULT>That's what he charged?

250
00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:27.320
<v TOWNER>Yes.

251
00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:30.520
<v ARSENEAULT>How would he do it?

252
00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:35.000
Just put it on a buggy?

253
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.973
<v TOWNER>How would he get the water out?

254
00:20:37.973 --> 00:20:39.247
With a bucket.

255
00:20:39.247 --> 00:20:48.589
He had some kind of thing rigged up on a tree and it was a . . . and then he had a long board,

256
00:20:48.589 --> 00:20:52.836
I think, on it with a rope and a bucket.

257
00:20:52.836 --> 00:20:57.507
And he would . . . How in the heck would he do that?

258
00:20:57.507 --> 00:21:03.452
I don't know, but it was some kind of way he would work that.

259
00:21:03.452 --> 00:21:11.521
Like an arm to dip that water out and then bring it out and pour it in the barrel.

260
00:21:11.521 --> 00:21:15.767
And that's . . . Yes, that's the way he got it out.

261
00:21:15.767 --> 00:21:22.986
And he'd dip it out bucket by bucket, I guess, when he got it to the house.

262
00:21:22.986 --> 00:21:30.630
Darn, I should have asked my brothers all of these questions because their memory is better than mine.

263
00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:32.753
<v ARSENEAULT>I can interview them, too.

264
00:21:32.753 --> 00:21:34.452
<v TOWNER>I doubt that [laughs].

265
00:21:34.452 --> 00:21:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They don't want to talk?

266
00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:40.882
<v TOWNER>No. No, I don't think they would.

267
00:21:40.882 --> 00:21:47.538
<v ARSENEAULT>I'll just have to come back and do another one with you.

268
00:21:47.538 --> 00:21:50.311
<v TOWNER>And I'll have them here.

269
00:21:50.311 --> 00:21:55.857
There's a lot of things they remember and I don't,

270
00:21:55.857 --> 00:22:04.176
<c.p>because my baby brother, he lived a lot with our grandmother that lived in Westlake.

271
00:22:04.176 --> 00:22:15.824
And then Walter was hanging around with my grandfather, because he had horses and things like that and Walter loved them.

272
00:22:15.824 --> 00:22:21.370
<v ARSENEAULT>So your grandfather who had the garden, he also had . . .

273
00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:25.252
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Horses, cows, all of that . . . chickens.

274
00:22:25.252 --> 00:22:28.580
Yes, he had all of that.

275
00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:35.790
Whatever it took to live, that's what he had, that's what he raised.

276
00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:43.000
And my grandmother would can some of it that he didn't sell.

277
00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.951
<v ARSENEAULT>How would he sell it?

278
00:22:44.951 --> 00:22:46.122
<v TOWNER>I don't know.

279
00:22:46.122 --> 00:22:47.293
In the bunch.

280
00:22:47.293 --> 00:22:55.098
If it was some type of grain, he'd sell that in a bunch like you get in the markets now.

281
00:22:55.098 --> 00:22:59.390
And the watermelons just like they are now, by the piece.

282
00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:01.732
Oh, and he sold sweet potatoes.

283
00:23:01.732 --> 00:23:04.073
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he sell them in Mossville?

284
00:23:04.073 --> 00:23:07.195
<v TOWNER>Mossville, Westlake, all . . . that was just an area.

285
00:23:07.195 --> 00:23:07.976
And Sulphur.

286
00:23:07.976 --> 00:23:10.707
He had a horse and wagon [laughs].

287
00:23:10.707 --> 00:23:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He sold it off the horse and off the wagon?

288
00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:15.718
<v TOWNER>Yes.

289
00:23:15.718 --> 00:23:19.305
That's how he sold it.

290
00:23:19.305 --> 00:23:20.740
Oh, shucks.

291
00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:25.763
He had a horse and a wagon.

292
00:23:25.763 --> 00:23:29.350
He was living good [laughs].

293
00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:42.983
The women would just about know what day he was coming by, and they'd be looking out for him.

294
00:23:42.983 --> 00:23:44.418
Oh, shucks.

295
00:23:44.418 --> 00:23:54.463
But then during those days I think everybody had some type of little garden.

296
00:23:54.463 --> 00:23:58.768
But he had a big one.

297
00:23:58.768 --> 00:24:08.096
And I think he raised hay for his horses and all of that.

298
00:24:08.096 --> 00:24:21.729
I can't think of the store in Westlake or the one in Sulphur that they did their grocery shopping.

299
00:24:21.729 --> 00:24:28.186
Like, that was an all day job on Saturdays.

300
00:24:28.186 --> 00:24:39.667
Grandfather would hook up the horses, hook up the wagon and they'd go to the store.

301
00:24:39.667 --> 00:24:44.689
And that was an all day job.

302
00:24:44.689 --> 00:24:54.017
It would be a couple of women that would go at one time.

303
00:24:54.017 --> 00:24:58.322
<v ARSENEAULT>He would take people into town?

304
00:24:58.322 --> 00:25:04.780
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, downtown Sulphur down by the railroad track.

305
00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:12.672
Darn, I can't . . . I don't know the name of that store.

306
00:25:12.672 --> 00:25:16.977
But that was the main stop.

307
00:25:16.977 --> 00:25:22.000
But all of that's changed now.

308
00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:27.366
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things are they getting at the store?

309
00:25:27.366 --> 00:25:31.122
<v TOWNER>Like cold cuts and meat, fresh meat.

310
00:25:31.122 --> 00:25:35.415
Maybe something that they didn't have already smoked.

311
00:25:35.415 --> 00:25:39.171
But they didn't have no deep freezer.

312
00:25:39.171 --> 00:25:41.854
No, they'd smoke their meat.

313
00:25:41.854 --> 00:25:46.683
My grandfather used to smoke ours with my grandmothers.

314
00:25:46.683 --> 00:25:56.878
And they had hogs, cows, and they'd butcher at a certain time of the year and cure that meat.

315
00:25:56.878 --> 00:26:03.317
And it would last for a long time, until the next killing.

316
00:26:03.317 --> 00:26:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember it?

317
00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:07.867
<v TOWNER>Yes, some of it.

318
00:26:07.867 --> 00:26:12.067
I got a picture in there . . . what was it?

319
00:26:12.067 --> 00:26:15.800
My sister-in-law or somebody in there cleaning chitterlings.

320
00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:19.533
<v ARSENEAULT>Now, just for the record, what are chitterlings?

321
00:26:19.533 --> 00:26:22.333
<v TOWNER>That's the insides of a hog.

322
00:26:22.333 --> 00:26:23.733
Yes, really nasty.

323
00:26:23.733 --> 00:26:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What would y'all do with them?

324
00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.595
<v TOWNER>Cook them.

325
00:26:28.595 --> 00:26:35.772
You talking about smell . . . that was an awful smell.

326
00:26:35.772 --> 00:26:39.759
That was an awful smell.

327
00:26:39.759 --> 00:26:41.354
<v ARSENEAULT>The chitterlings?

328
00:26:41.354 --> 00:26:44.544
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, back then.

329
00:26:44.544 --> 00:26:50.127
Now you have to pay for them.

330
00:26:50.127 --> 00:26:54.911
They used to give them away.

331
00:26:54.911 --> 00:26:58.101
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would butcher the . . .

332
00:26:58.101 --> 00:26:59.696
<v TOWNER>My grandfather.

333
00:26:59.696 --> 00:27:05.278
Then my husband's father, they would butcher.

334
00:27:05.278 --> 00:27:15.646
They didn't have any beef, but they had hogs and they would butcher.

335
00:27:15.646 --> 00:27:30.000
They had a special time of year, but I done forgot what time of year it was.

336
00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.681
<v ARSENEAULT>Butchering season.

337
00:27:31.681 --> 00:27:33.362
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes.

338
00:27:33.362 --> 00:27:38.404
So it wasn't . . . Like I say,

339
00:27:38.404 --> 00:27:51.851
it wasn't too bad if . . . And when they butchered, neighbors got meat and all of that.

340
00:27:51.851 --> 00:28:10.340
What was left over . . . Well when my grandfather would butcher he would smoke it, like hams, and they'd make their own sausage.

341
00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:15.383
And what else did they make?

342
00:28:15.383 --> 00:28:19.585
I think that's about it.

343
00:28:19.585 --> 00:28:30.511
Just the sausage, and the hams, and then roasts and things like that.

344
00:28:30.511 --> 00:28:39.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there any part of the pig that they didn't use?

345
00:28:39.755 --> 00:28:49.000
<v TOWNER>No. From the tail to the snout and the nose.

346
00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:51.442
<v ARSENEAULT>They used it all.

347
00:28:51.442 --> 00:28:52.664
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes.

348
00:28:52.664 --> 00:28:55.106
<v ARSENEAULT>Nothing went to waste.

349
00:28:55.106 --> 00:28:59.381
<v TOWNER>No. Well they'd pickle the pig feet.

350
00:28:59.381 --> 00:29:08.540
They'd cut those off and put them in vinegar and something else and pickle them.

351
00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:13.425
They had to sit there for a while.

352
00:29:13.425 --> 00:29:25.027
My grandmother did quite a bit of . . . I don't know if it was storing food or what it was.

353
00:29:25.027 --> 00:29:31.133
All I know is during the winter months everybody ate.

354
00:29:31.133 --> 00:29:36.018
So like I say, it wasn't too bad.

355
00:29:36.018 --> 00:29:38.460
At least we weren't.

356
00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:42.124
Oh, and my grandfather made moonshine.

357
00:29:42.124 --> 00:29:43.345
Sure did.

358
00:29:43.345 --> 00:29:50.062
Even the police used to come by and buy a bottle.

359
00:29:50.062 --> 00:29:50.673
I'm

360
00:29:50.673 --> 00:29:51.894
<c.p>not kidding.

361
00:29:51.894 --> 00:29:58.000
You see, he made that good stuff and the . . .

362
00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Was this during prohibition?

363
00:29:59.952 --> 00:30:06.298
<v TOWNER>No. This was just back in the . . . I guess the '30s and '40s.

364
00:30:06.298 --> 00:30:11.667
And one of them was the head policeman in Lake Charles.

365
00:30:11.667 --> 00:30:17.036
They’d say . . . Well his dad come out there and buy alcohol.

366
00:30:17.036 --> 00:30:18.988
<v ARSENEAULT>How'd he make it?

367
00:30:18.988 --> 00:30:23.381
<v TOWNER>I have no . . . I wish I did know. [laughs]

368
00:30:23.381 --> 00:30:25.333
<v ARSENEAULT>It's a secret recipe.

369
00:30:25.333 --> 00:30:30.214
<v TOWNER>No. He didn't write no recipe or nothing like that.

370
00:30:30.214 --> 00:30:32.167
I have no idea.

371
00:30:32.167 --> 00:30:33.143
None whatsoever.

372
00:30:33.143 --> 00:30:36.560
Next time you see Butch, ask him.

373
00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:39.000
He might know. [laughs]

374
00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:40.846
<v ARSENEAULT>One of his customers?

375
00:30:40.846 --> 00:30:43.615
<v TOWNER>Oh, no. Butch was too young.

376
00:30:43.615 --> 00:30:45.923
But yeah, he made moonshine.

377
00:30:45.923 --> 00:30:50.538
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: My Mama moved what, in 1956, '55 . . .

378
00:30:50.538 --> 00:30:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What?

379
00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:54.231
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: . . . you moved to California?

380
00:30:54.231 --> 00:30:55.154
<v TOWNER>No! '61.

381
00:30:55.154 --> 00:30:58.739
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: I wasn't even there yet.

382
00:30:58.739 --> 00:31:01.870
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: But you know, it just sucks.

383
00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:05.000
Oh, here comes some other matriarchs of Mossville.

384
00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:05.372
<v TOWNER>Who?

385
00:31:05.372 --> 00:31:09.837
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: Your daughter and your cousin . . . I mean, your niece.

386
00:31:09.837 --> 00:31:13.558
Now you see them two, they can tell you something.

387
00:31:13.558 --> 00:31:13.930
<v TOWNER>Who?

388
00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:16.163
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: [Debbie?] and [Chucky?].

389
00:31:16.163 --> 00:31:16.535
<v TOWNER>Oh.

390
00:31:16.535 --> 00:31:18.767
<v ARSENEAULT>Do we need to pause it?

391
00:31:18.767 --> 00:31:19.512
<v TOWNER>You can.

392
00:31:19.512 --> 00:31:22.667
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: That [Baybay?], she'll tell you a whole bunch of stories.

393
00:31:22.667 --> 00:31:23.500
<v TOWNER>They done talked to [Baybay?].

394
00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:24.333
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Oh really?

395
00:31:24.333 --> 00:31:24.533
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

396
00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:26.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: And they still got ears?

397
00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.080
<v TOWNER>Oh wait a minute.

398
00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:29.120
Lenoria Ambrose.

399
00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.720
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: That's [Baybay?].

400
00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:34.320
She got about twenty names.

401
00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.440
<v ARSENEAULT>You want to pause it, Jamie?

402
00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:37.960
<v DIGILORMO>Sure.

403
00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

404
00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:41.069
<v LENORIA AMBROSE>Because who was James?

405
00:31:41.069 --> 00:31:43.655
That was Jasper Moss's dad.

406
00:31:43.655 --> 00:31:44.690
Wasn't it?

407
00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:47.276
<v TOWNER>Oh okay, then that was . . .

408
00:31:47.276 --> 00:31:48.310
<v AMBROSE>[ . . . ?] Jack's daddy?

409
00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:48.828
James?

410
00:31:48.828 --> 00:31:51.931
<v TOWNER>I thought his name was Jack.

411
00:31:51.931 --> 00:31:52.966
<v AMBROSE>Oh, Jack?

412
00:31:52.966 --> 00:31:54.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

413
00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:57.846
<v AMBROSE>Oh well then they both are probably from James Moss.

414
00:31:57.846 --> 00:32:02.077
I mean, they gave them . . . James Moss gave them the property.

415
00:32:02.077 --> 00:32:05.154
So maybe James Moss was Jack Moss's brother.

416
00:32:05.154 --> 00:32:07.462
<v TOWNER>Well it would have to be.

417
00:32:07.462 --> 00:32:08.615
Everybody was related.

418
00:32:08.615 --> 00:32:09.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

419
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:16.692
Because we're trying to figure out how granny . . . because she was a Moss and she was part of that family.

420
00:32:16.692 --> 00:32:24.385
But we don't know exactly who . . . if they were her brothers, because [Web?] Moss, lived with she and [Isom?] Perkins.

421
00:32:24.385 --> 00:32:28.231
When they got married and he lived in their home.

422
00:32:28.231 --> 00:32:29.000
<v TOWNER>Who?

423
00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:29.769
<v AMBROSE>[Web?] Moss.

424
00:32:29.769 --> 00:32:30.538
<v TOWNER>Oh, okay.

425
00:32:30.538 --> 00:32:34.385
<v AMBROSE>And that's who Daddy bought his property from, [Web?] Moss.

426
00:32:34.385 --> 00:32:34.769
<v TOWNER>Okay.

427
00:32:34.769 --> 00:32:37.846
<v AMBROSE>And [Web?] Moss was one of the deacons . . .

428
00:32:37.846 --> 00:32:39.000
<v TOWNER>At the . . .

429
00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:40.182
<v AMBROSE>. . . at Mount Zion.

430
00:32:40.182 --> 00:32:42.152
One of the first deacons.

431
00:32:42.152 --> 00:32:43.727
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: Oh!

432
00:32:43.727 --> 00:32:46.091
That should have been nineteen . . . what?

433
00:32:46.091 --> 00:32:46.485
<v AMBROSE>Eighteen.

434
00:32:46.485 --> 00:32:51.606
So don't know whether [Web?] Moss was her brother or who he was.

435
00:32:51.606 --> 00:32:58.303
We know it was Ken, but we don't know if that was a nephew or a brother.

436
00:32:58.303 --> 00:32:59.879
<v TOWNER>I have no idea.

437
00:32:59.879 --> 00:33:05.000
<v AMBROSE>So Jack and James was part of that same family, granny's family.

438
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:05.381
<v TOWNER>Yes.

439
00:33:05.381 --> 00:33:09.190
<v AMBROSE>But we don't know if they were sisters or brothers.

440
00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:11.857
<v TOWNER>I think Jack Moss was paw paw's . . .

441
00:33:11.857 --> 00:33:12.238
<v AMBROSE>Dad.

442
00:33:12.238 --> 00:33:12.619
<v TOWNER>. . . Daddy.

443
00:33:12.619 --> 00:33:16.810
And Uncle Jack and the rest of the brothers and sisters . . .

444
00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:20.238
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: [loud screech] It ain't going nowhere.

445
00:33:20.238 --> 00:33:21.000
<v AMBROSE>Oh.

446
00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:22.478
<v TOWNER>Go back in there!

447
00:33:22.478 --> 00:33:26.913
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: I just wanted to get you a little love.

448
00:33:26.913 --> 00:33:30.609
I want to listen to what you guys talking about.

449
00:33:30.609 --> 00:33:33.196
<v TOWNER>We ain’t going to say nothing. [laughter]

450
00:33:33.196 --> 00:33:34.674
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: See.

451
00:33:34.674 --> 00:33:36.522
<v DIGILORMO>That's how they do you.

452
00:33:36.522 --> 00:33:38.725
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Yes, exactly.

453
00:33:38.725 --> 00:33:39.449
<v TOWNER>Oh, yes.

454
00:33:39.449 --> 00:33:40.899
I have no idea . . .

455
00:33:40.899 --> 00:33:51.043
<v AMBROSE>But that's why we were trying to find some papers or . . . I guess I'm going to look and see if the church . . . if they have the church deed,

456
00:33:51.043 --> 00:33:55.029
and see what I can figure out on the church deed.

457
00:33:55.029 --> 00:34:00.464
I've got to ask Ricky if he knows where it is, because it may have . . .

458
00:34:00.464 --> 00:34:03.000
<v TOWNER>Who gave the church their property?

459
00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:04.000
<v AMBROSE>James Moss.

460
00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:05.000
<v TOWNER>James Moss.

461
00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:05.500
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

462
00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:07.000
<v TOWNER>Oh, Jim Moss.

463
00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:08.000
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees]

464
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.778
<v TOWNER>That was Pete's daddy.

465
00:34:09.778 --> 00:34:12.889
<v ARSENEAULT>Who owned . . . who had the post office?

466
00:34:12.889 --> 00:34:13.778
<v AMBROSE>The [Purdys?].

467
00:34:13.778 --> 00:34:14.667
<v TOWNER>Post office?

468
00:34:14.667 --> 00:34:18.222
<v AMBROSE>Oh, the Post office . . . that was a Rigmaiden.

469
00:34:18.222 --> 00:34:23.111
