632
00:43:29.264 --> 00:43:31.655
<v TOWNER>She didn't have Joy Hill then?

633
00:43:31.655 --> 00:43:34.047
<v AMBROSE>Joy Hill was on the corner.

634
00:43:34.047 --> 00:43:34.845
Wasn’t it?

635
00:43:34.845 --> 00:43:36.041
<v TOWNER>I don’t know.

636
00:43:36.041 --> 00:43:40.426
<v AMBROSE>It was on the corner of Trousdale and Old Spanish Trail.

637
00:43:40.426 --> 00:43:43.216
They call her place the swamp house.

638
00:43:43.216 --> 00:43:46.007
Now she might have had Joy Hill.

639
00:43:46.007 --> 00:43:48.000
<v TOWNER>She had Joy Hill.

640
00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:49.273
<v AMBROSE>Well, on that corner.

641
00:43:49.273 --> 00:43:49.591
<v TOWNER>Yes.

642
00:43:49.591 --> 00:43:49.909
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

643
00:43:49.909 --> 00:43:54.045
But he told me that Joy Hill was owned by a white man.

644
00:43:54.045 --> 00:43:55.000
<v TOWNER>Joy Hill?

645
00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:55.889
<v AMBROSE>[Sir Reese?].

646
00:43:55.889 --> 00:44:07.000
Yes, he told me that it was a white fellow that owned it, but it was black patrons, and it was always run by blacks.

647
00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:09.222
<v TOWNER>Carl Ray told you that?

648
00:44:09.222 --> 00:44:09.667
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees]

649
00:44:09.667 --> 00:44:10.111
<v TOWNER>Really?

650
00:44:10.111 --> 00:44:11.000
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees]

651
00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:12.324
<v TOWNER>Well he'd know.

652
00:44:12.324 --> 00:44:21.588
<v AMBROSE>But then, of course, because of Patty, I guess she worked there, or took it over, or rented it or something.

653
00:44:21.588 --> 00:44:22.912
<v TOWNER>I don't know.

654
00:44:22.912 --> 00:44:31.294
But Ms. Patty had it for a long time, because that [ . . . ?], they was running it and stole one night.

655
00:44:31.294 --> 00:44:33.941
Stole more money from that woman.

656
00:44:33.941 --> 00:44:39.235
And that's how they went to Galveston [Texas] on a bus trip.

657
00:44:39.235 --> 00:44:41.000
<v AMBROSE>Lord have mercy.

658
00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:42.333
<v TOWNER>Sure did. [laughs]

659
00:44:42.333 --> 00:44:45.000
<v AMBROSE>Because Ms. Patty paid for it.

660
00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:45.444
<v TOWNER>Yes.

661
00:44:45.444 --> 00:44:49.444
<v AMBROSE>But I remember those families on that . . . on Trousdale.

662
00:44:49.444 --> 00:44:53.000
<v TOWNER>Darn, I can't remember who it was.

663
00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:58.302
<v AMBROSE>But I don't know why I said he was . . . there was some [Reese?] was the owner who owned it.

664
00:44:58.302 --> 00:45:01.093
<v TOWNER>I'm going to call [Tegan?] and see if he'll remember.

665
00:45:01.093 --> 00:45:01.372
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

666
00:45:01.372 --> 00:45:02.209
He may remember.

667
00:45:02.209 --> 00:45:03.884
<v TOWNER>You can't remember somebody called T.

668
00:45:03.884 --> 00:45:05.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes, that's true.

669
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:05.960
<v ARSENEAULT>Who's T?

670
00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:06.920
<v AMBROSE>Thomas Porter.

671
00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:08.840
Have you interviewed him?

672
00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:09.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Not yet.

673
00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:14.120
I saw his paintings, though . . . some of his paintings.

674
00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:16.040
<v AMBROSE>That was Johnny Comeaux's . . .

675
00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

676
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:17.913
<v AMBROSE>. . . paintings you saw.

677
00:45:17.913 --> 00:45:19.435
T doesn't paint, does he?

678
00:45:19.435 --> 00:45:20.957
<v TOWNER>Not that I know of.

679
00:45:20.957 --> 00:45:22.783
<v AMBROSE>I never knew him to paint.

680
00:45:22.783 --> 00:45:23.391
<v TOWNER>He decorates.

681
00:45:23.391 --> 00:45:24.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

682
00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:26.024
<v TOWNER>Yes, he used to decorate.

683
00:45:26.024 --> 00:45:28.857
<v AMBROSE>But I've never known him to paint.

684
00:45:28.857 --> 00:45:31.690
But I bet that was Johnny Comeaux.

685
00:45:31.690 --> 00:45:34.119
Where'd you see his paintings at?

686
00:45:34.119 --> 00:45:35.333
His daughter's house?

687
00:45:35.333 --> 00:45:36.548
<v ARSENEAULT>The Mossville reunion.

688
00:45:36.548 --> 00:45:36.952
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

689
00:45:36.952 --> 00:45:38.167
That was Johnny.

690
00:45:38.167 --> 00:45:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He's a Mossville painter, Johnny Comeaux?

691
00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:41.368
<v TOWNER>Really?

692
00:45:41.368 --> 00:45:41.737
<v AMBROSE>Johnny?

693
00:45:41.737 --> 00:45:42.474
Oh yes.

694
00:45:42.474 --> 00:45:48.737
They had some of his paintings here in . . . in fact, I had something out of the paper.

695
00:45:48.737 --> 00:45:52.053
I'll have to see if I can find it.

696
00:45:52.053 --> 00:45:56.842
He was displayed here over on . . . what is the old first ward school?

697
00:45:56.842 --> 00:45:58.316
<v TOWNER>[?] ever made it back?

698
00:45:58.316 --> 00:46:00.526
<v UNIDENTIFIED MALE>No, I’m fixing to call her.

699
00:46:00.526 --> 00:46:03.474
She got to be at work for three.

700
00:46:03.474 --> 00:46:04.579
<v TOWNER>I know it.

701
00:46:04.579 --> 00:46:06.053
<v AMBROSE>Here in Lake Charles?

702
00:46:06.053 --> 00:46:07.895
His paintings were on display.

703
00:46:07.895 --> 00:46:11.947
He started painting . . . I didn't know in school that he painted.

704
00:46:11.947 --> 00:46:14.895
He was one of our star basketball players.

705
00:46:14.895 --> 00:46:24.105
And he went off and went to the military and when he got out the military he started . . . he never came back home to stay.

706
00:46:24.105 --> 00:46:30.000
But he started painting, and his paintings are quite expensive from what I understand now.

707
00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.339
<v TOWNER>Oh, I didn't know that.

708
00:46:32.339 --> 00:46:38.887
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: Grandpa built some kind of [Pelican Inn?] motel on Highway 90.

709
00:46:38.887 --> 00:46:39.823
<v TOWNER>[?], go away.

710
00:46:39.823 --> 00:46:45.903
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: I'm just giving you some history my daddy told me.

711
00:46:45.903 --> 00:46:54.323
<v AMBROSE>Your grandpa built a whole bunch of things around here, from Deridder to the Port of Lake Charles.

712
00:46:54.323 --> 00:46:56.661
He's talking about my grandfather.

713
00:46:56.661 --> 00:46:58.065
He was a . . .

714
00:46:58.065 --> 00:46:59.000
<v TOWNER>Carpenter.

715
00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:00.833
<v AMBROSE>. . . carpenter and worked for contractors.

716
00:47:00.833 --> 00:47:02.667
He was responsible over on

717
00:47:02.667 --> 00:47:08.533
<c.p>the lake front where they have all these mansions and everything, and out at Big Lake.

718
00:47:08.533 --> 00:47:14.400
He built a dock that's still there today where they would moor their boats and things.

719
00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:17.700
Daddy . . . my grandfather helped build that . . . well, built it.

720
00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:25.400
And I think his name is in the . . . Butch said he was going to take me down there to see it.

721
00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:26.133
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

722
00:47:26.133 --> 00:47:29.067
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] His name is etched in the concrete.

723
00:47:29.067 --> 00:47:29.800
<v TOWNER>Oh wow.

724
00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:31.267
I didn't know that.

725
00:47:31.267 --> 00:47:32.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

726
00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:34.807
<v TOWNER>[ . . . ?] there's a lot of things I don't know.

727
00:47:34.807 --> 00:47:39.018
<v AMBROSE>Well you start talking, you start talking about all those old things.

728
00:47:39.018 --> 00:47:43.228
But okay, then Jim Moss has . . . getting back to the post office.

729
00:47:43.228 --> 00:47:49.895
If Jim Moss had the post office and it was probably down on our end somewhere where he lived.

730
00:47:49.895 --> 00:47:50.246
<v TOWNER>No.

731
00:47:50.246 --> 00:47:52.000
<v AMBROSE>Where did he live?

732
00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:57.040
<v TOWNER>The post office is down there by . . . You don't remember uncle Garrett . . . I mean . . .

733
00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:57.760
<v AMBROSE>Ira Garrett.

734
00:47:57.760 --> 00:47:59.200
<v TOWNER>. . . Ira Garrett, do you?

735
00:47:59.200 --> 00:47:59.920
<v AMBROSE>Well, yes.

736
00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:01.000
<v TOWNER>You do?

737
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:02.750
<v AMBROSE>Yes, across the . . . by the railroad track.

738
00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:03.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

739
00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:03.250
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

740
00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:04.750
<v TOWNER>That's where the post office was.

741
00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:05.000
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

742
00:48:05.000 --> 00:48:07.000
So that's where he had it then.

743
00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:07.500
<v TOWNER>Yes.

744
00:48:07.500 --> 00:48:13.500
<v AMBROSE>And that would be ideal, because it's right by the train tracks.

745
00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:14.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

746
00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:16.500
<v AMBROSE>That would have been ideal.

747
00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:29.000
Well we had quite a few stores in Mossville . . . canteens and . . . Because the ones I remember was Ira Garrett's store and Martill had a store,

748
00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:32.000
Jake Rigmaiden had a store.

749
00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:33.786
<v TOWNER>Jake Rigmaiden had a store?

750
00:48:33.786 --> 00:48:36.643
<v AMBROSE>Yes, right there in front of their house.

751
00:48:36.643 --> 00:48:42.357
Yes, to the west of their house, right in front of where those apartments are . . . were.

752
00:48:42.357 --> 00:48:44.143
Right in front of there.

753
00:48:44.143 --> 00:48:45.214
<v TOWNER>That ain't Jake.

754
00:48:45.214 --> 00:48:45.929
<v AMBROSE>Not Jake.

755
00:48:45.929 --> 00:48:47.000
<v TOWNER>That was . . .

756
00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:48.114
<v AMBROSE>That was Josh.

757
00:48:48.114 --> 00:48:50.343
<v TOWNER>Girl, you getting me all confused.

758
00:48:50.343 --> 00:48:51.086
<v AMBROSE>I'm sorry.

759
00:48:51.086 --> 00:48:51.829
Josh Rigmaiden.

760
00:48:51.829 --> 00:48:54.429
I'm always saying Jake, but it's Josh.

761
00:48:54.429 --> 00:48:55.171
Josh Rigmaiden.

762
00:48:55.171 --> 00:48:55.543
<v TOWNER>Yes.

763
00:48:55.543 --> 00:48:57.029
<v AMBROSE>He had a store.

764
00:48:57.029 --> 00:48:58.143
And who else?

765
00:48:58.143 --> 00:49:00.000
Ms. [?] had a store.

766
00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:00.898
<v TOWNER>Yes.

767
00:49:00.898 --> 00:49:06.286
And those people on Prater Road.

768
00:49:06.286 --> 00:49:12.571
<v AMBROSE>The . . . I know who you're talking about.

769
00:49:12.571 --> 00:49:16.163
What was their name?

770
00:49:16.163 --> 00:49:20.653
They had a Creole name.

771
00:49:20.653 --> 00:49:26.041
Because they sold meat and everything.

772
00:49:26.041 --> 00:49:26.939
Rougeau’s.

773
00:49:26.939 --> 00:49:27.837
<v TOWNER>Yes.

774
00:49:27.837 --> 00:49:29.633
<v AMBROSE>The Rougeau’s.

775
00:49:29.633 --> 00:49:33.224
Those were the stores.

776
00:49:33.224 --> 00:49:41.306
Well, I guess Aunt Martill’s wasn't really a store.

777
00:49:41.306 --> 00:49:44.000
<v TOWNER>Was what?

778
00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:48.803
<v AMBROSE>Aunt Martill’s, was that really a store or more a canteen?

779
00:49:48.803 --> 00:49:50.986
<v TOWNER>No, it was a store.

780
00:49:50.986 --> 00:49:54.042
<v AMBROSE>She had meat and everything in there?

781
00:49:54.042 --> 00:49:56.662
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, it was a store.

782
00:49:56.662 --> 00:49:59.718
<v AMBROSE>And then cousin [Edie?] had a canteen.

783
00:49:59.718 --> 00:50:03.211
And [Martine?] had a little canteen and barbershop.

784
00:50:03.211 --> 00:50:05.394
Ms. Freelow had a canteen.

785
00:50:05.394 --> 00:50:06.268
<v TOWNER>Ms. who?

786
00:50:06.268 --> 00:50:15.000
<v AMBROSE>Ms. Freelow . . . the Freelow’s, that's where we used to go slip off of campus and go to her canteen.

787
00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:16.935
<v TOWNER>Well that was after '61.

788
00:50:16.935 --> 00:50:18.097
And Ms. Lula.

789
00:50:18.097 --> 00:50:25.839
<v AMBROSE>Ms. Lula Lyons, she had the first big canteen, and that was right across the street from the swimming pool.

790
00:50:25.839 --> 00:50:27.000
<v TOWNER>School, yes.

791
00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:28.726
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was Purdy's?

792
00:50:28.726 --> 00:50:35.055
<v TOWNER>It was across from Bel Air, right around that Trousdale Road.

793
00:50:35.055 --> 00:50:38.507
It was over in that area.

794
00:50:38.507 --> 00:50:43.110
<v ARSENEAULT>You were the first one to work there?

795
00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:46.562
<v TOWNER>Me and . . . Well my aunt, yes.

796
00:50:46.562 --> 00:50:48.288
<v AMBROSE>Gladys Marie Perkins . . .

797
00:50:48.288 --> 00:50:52.315
<v TOWNER>Girl, my name ain't no Gladys. [laughs]

798
00:50:52.315 --> 00:50:52.890
<v AMBROSE>. . . Towner.

799
00:50:52.890 --> 00:51:00.370
The first employee of Purdy's Drug Store, along with her aunt, Anna Williams,

800
00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:09.000
where she spent all of her money on comic books and true confessions [laughs].

801
00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:09.796
<v TOWNER>[laughs] Yes.

802
00:51:09.796 --> 00:51:12.184
<v AMBROSE>I thought that was so funny.

803
00:51:12.184 --> 00:51:12.980
Oh, gosh.

804
00:51:12.980 --> 00:51:22.531
And then I think I told you my thing that my dad had the first . . . he had a grocery store down by Bel Air,

805
00:51:22.531 --> 00:51:26.908
and he had a washateria, a laundry, and a furniture store.

806
00:51:26.908 --> 00:51:30.092
I didn't . . . I don't remember the furniture store.

807
00:51:30.092 --> 00:51:36.459
But everybody tells me that he had . . . And I don't know why I don't remember that.

808
00:51:36.459 --> 00:51:38.449
I remember the grocery store.

809
00:51:38.449 --> 00:51:39.245
<v TOWNER>That's right.

810
00:51:39.245 --> 00:51:41.633
You know, I forgot about that.

811
00:51:41.633 --> 00:51:44.418
Mr. [Picard?] had that first, didn't he?

812
00:51:44.418 --> 00:51:45.214
<v AMBROSE>Did he?

813
00:51:45.214 --> 00:51:47.204
That's what someone told me.

814
00:51:47.204 --> 00:51:48.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

815
00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:49.412
<v AMBROSE>The grocery store.

816
00:51:49.412 --> 00:51:49.882
<v TOWNER>Yes.

817
00:51:49.882 --> 00:51:50.824
That's right.

818
00:51:50.824 --> 00:51:52.235
Mother worked there.

819
00:51:52.235 --> 00:51:52.706
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

820
00:51:52.706 --> 00:51:53.176
<v TOWNER>Darn.

821
00:51:53.176 --> 00:51:56.000
Mother had two jobs then. [laughs]

822
00:51:56.000 --> 00:52:04.000
<v AMBROSE>He had . . . And I don't remember what kind of furniture he had because I don't remember . . .

823
00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:06.386
<v TOWNER>Because it wasn't that big.

824
00:52:06.386 --> 00:52:09.250
<v AMBROSE>No. Well it was next door.

825
00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:09.727
<v TOWNER>Yes.

826
00:52:09.727 --> 00:52:18.795
<v AMBROSE>The store was here across the street that separated, I guess, their house from the . . . was the furniture store.

827
00:52:18.795 --> 00:52:21.182
It was two separate buildings.

828
00:52:21.182 --> 00:52:25.000
<v TOWNER>Yes, but they didn't live down there.

829
00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:28.844
<v AMBROSE>They lived behind the grocery store in some apartments.

830
00:52:28.844 --> 00:52:30.125
There was a

831
00:52:30.125 --> 00:52:35.250
<c.p>house and then some apartments that EJ and his wife stayed in.

832
00:52:35.250 --> 00:52:35.677
<v TOWNER>Yes.

833
00:52:35.677 --> 00:52:43.792
<v AMBROSE>Mr. Williams, Mary Jean's daddy, his wife . . . he and his wife . . . I don't remember what his first name was.

834
00:52:43.792 --> 00:52:46.781
But he was a Williams. [ . . . ?] Mary Jean.

835
00:52:46.781 --> 00:52:49.771
They were those apartments in the back.

836
00:52:49.771 --> 00:52:54.469
<v TOWNER>Yes, I know . . . I remember the apartment, but I don't remember . . .

837
00:52:54.469 --> 00:52:57.885
<v AMBROSE>And he had a house right back here.

838
00:52:57.885 --> 00:53:06.000
That's where I had to used to go to leave from Sunday school from church and go there.

839
00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:09.714
<v TOWNER>[laughs] Oh, because you know they had a house next door to us.

840
00:53:09.714 --> 00:53:12.286
<v AMBROSE>That was the house I remember most of all.

841
00:53:12.286 --> 00:53:16.286
But this was . . . I don't know if this was before that house or after,

842
00:53:16.286 --> 00:53:20.286
but I know I had to leave church and go down to that house.

843
00:53:20.286 --> 00:53:20.857
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

844
00:53:20.857 --> 00:53:21.143
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

845
00:53:21.143 --> 00:53:22.000
<v TOWNER>Mount Zion?

846
00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:22.533
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

847
00:53:22.533 --> 00:53:25.200
<v TOWNER>You didn't walk, did you?

848
00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:25.733
<v AMBROSE>No.

849
00:53:25.733 --> 00:53:26.800
<v TOWNER>Oh, okay.

850
00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:29.467
<v AMBROSE>He brought me down there.

851
00:53:29.467 --> 00:53:36.933
And then he was the first black to run for police juror, Gradis Braxton.

852
00:53:36.933 --> 00:53:46.000
And then Ms. [Reesa Mae?], did they have a store or was it just a canteen?

853
00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:48.483
<v TOWNER>See that was after I left.

854
00:53:48.483 --> 00:53:50.552
<v AMBROSE>Down there in Lincoln Heights?

855
00:53:50.552 --> 00:53:52.621
<v TOWNER>That was after I left.

856
00:53:52.621 --> 00:53:54.276
<v AMBROSE>[Reesie?] and EJ Mouton.

857
00:53:54.276 --> 00:53:55.517
They had a . . .

858
00:53:55.517 --> 00:53:58.000
<v TOWNER>Who had the night club?

859
00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:03.056
<v AMBROSE>EJ and somebody were in partnership with a night club,

860
00:54:03.056 --> 00:54:11.652
and then Tisha and her husband had a night club . . . the same . . . that was in Bel Air.

861
00:54:11.652 --> 00:54:14.180
They were next door to

862
00:54:14.180 --> 00:54:15.191
<c.p>each other.

863
00:54:15.191 --> 00:54:20.247
<v ARSENEAULT>Do y'all remember any musicians who would come to Mossville?

864
00:54:20.247 --> 00:54:21.258
<v AMBROSE>Oh, God.

865
00:54:21.258 --> 00:54:26.315
That was at the Paradise, at Mr. Valery Montgomery's business.

866
00:54:26.315 --> 00:54:30.865
He had everybody from James Brown, Bobby Blue Bland . . .

867
00:54:30.865 --> 00:54:31.371
<v TOWNER>B.B.

868
00:54:31.371 --> 00:54:31.876
King.

869
00:54:31.876 --> 00:54:32.382
<v AMBROSE>B.B.

870
00:54:32.382 --> 00:54:32.888
King.

871
00:54:32.888 --> 00:54:34.404
What's his name?

872
00:54:34.404 --> 00:54:37.944
Anybody who was somebody during those times.

873
00:54:37.944 --> 00:54:40.472
That was during the '60s.

874
00:54:40.472 --> 00:54:43.000
<v TOWNER>Yes, the late '50s

875
00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:43.915
<v AMBROSE>'70s, '50s.

876
00:54:43.915 --> 00:54:53.064
I don't remember the '60s, because I always wanted to go, but was too young to get to see anybody.

877
00:54:53.064 --> 00:54:53.979
He had . . .

878
00:54:53.979 --> 00:54:55.351
<v TOWNER>Oh, Fats Domino . . .

879
00:54:55.351 --> 00:54:56.723
<v AMBROSE>Yes, Fats Domino.

880
00:54:56.723 --> 00:54:57.638
<v TOWNER>. . . was there.

881
00:54:57.638 --> 00:54:58.096
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

882
00:54:58.096 --> 00:54:59.011
<v AMBROSE>Chuck Berry.

883
00:54:59.011 --> 00:55:06.330
The same ones that they would have over at [Balls?] Auditorium they would have out there.

884
00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:08.617
And what was his name?

885
00:55:08.617 --> 00:55:10.904
He was so good looking.

886
00:55:10.904 --> 00:55:13.649
He was the best looking thing.

887
00:55:13.649 --> 00:55:16.851
Well, I’ll talk a little bit more.

888
00:55:16.851 --> 00:55:19.596
It will come to my mind.

889
00:55:19.596 --> 00:55:26.000
I’m trying to think of some other artists he had during those times.

890
00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:27.807
<v ARSENEAULT>How would he advertise the . . .

891
00:55:27.807 --> 00:55:33.952
<v AMBROSE>I'm sure he had it on . . . We had a radio station here called KLK in Lake Charles,

892
00:55:33.952 --> 00:55:37.928
and we had a man by the name of Bubba Lutcher.

893
00:55:37.928 --> 00:55:42.265
He was the biggest, hottest thing on the radio during those times.

894
00:55:42.265 --> 00:55:46.241
So I'm sure a lot of it was on the radio.

895
00:55:46.241 --> 00:55:47.325
No TV advertisements . . .

896
00:55:47.325 --> 00:55:47.687
<v TOWNER>No.

897
00:55:47.687 --> 00:55:48.771
<v AMBROSE>. . . during that time.

898
00:55:48.771 --> 00:55:49.855
<v AMBROSE>Maybe some had . . .

899
00:55:49.855 --> 00:55:50.578
<v TOWNER>No TV.

900
00:55:50.578 --> 00:55:52.747
<v AMBROSE>. . . been . . . Exactly . . . in the American Press.

901
00:55:52.747 --> 00:55:56.000
But most of them was through . . . via the . . .

902
00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:56.818
<v TOWNER>Word of mouth.

903
00:55:56.818 --> 00:55:57.091
<v AMBROSE>. . . radio.

904
00:55:57.091 --> 00:55:58.455
Word of mouth and radio.

905
00:55:58.455 --> 00:55:59.818
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a black newspaper?

906
00:55:59.818 --> 00:56:00.091
<v AMBROSE>No.

907
00:56:00.091 --> 00:56:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mossville have its own paper?

908
00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:07.255
<v AMBROSE>No. I don't remember ever having its own paper.

909
00:56:07.255 --> 00:56:13.679
We all read the . . . had the American Press delivered to us.

910
00:56:13.679 --> 00:56:27.693
And anything that happened, of course, in Mossville was in the American Press, or Mossville, Westlake, Sulphur, Vinton, they carried everything, all the news.

911
00:56:27.693 --> 00:56:34.701
Jackie Wilson, Otis Redding . . . those are some more people who played there.

912
00:56:34.701 --> 00:56:39.372
I don't remember Ray Charles ever being there.

913
00:56:39.372 --> 00:56:43.460
You remember Ray Charles ever being there?

914
00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:44.044
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees]

915
00:56:44.044 --> 00:56:50.467
<v AMBROSE>Millie Jackson, she was another one that was there . . . female artist.

916
00:56:50.467 --> 00:56:58.642
And I don't know if Clarence Carter . . . He had a lot of R&amp;B artists.

917
00:56:58.642 --> 00:57:06.818
There was some blues artists, but Bobby Blue Bland was the biggest . . . and B.B.

918
00:57:06.818 --> 00:57:10.905
King were the biggest that I remember.

919
00:57:10.905 --> 00:57:16.161
And Bobby Blue Bland was there quite a bit.

920
00:57:16.161 --> 00:57:19.664
He was there more than once.

921
00:57:19.664 --> 00:57:22.000
<v TOWNER>I think so.

922
00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:25.038
<v AMBROSE>He was there on a regular basis.

923
00:57:25.038 --> 00:57:25.906
Wilson Pickett.

924
00:57:25.906 --> 00:57:29.377
I'm trying to think who else was there.

925
00:57:29.377 --> 00:57:33.717
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did the artists stay when they were in town?

926
00:57:33.717 --> 00:57:35.019
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

927
00:57:35.019 --> 00:57:36.755
I assume that they . . .

928
00:57:36.755 --> 00:57:39.358
<v TOWNER>Probably over here in Lake Charles.

929
00:57:39.358 --> 00:57:43.264
<v AMBROSE>Somewhere in . . . Yeah, because we didn't have a . . . well,

930
00:57:43.264 --> 00:57:48.906
we had a motel at Mossville, but I'm sure they didn't stay there.

931
00:57:48.906 --> 00:57:58.887
They probably . . . they either stayed there or on their buses, or they came in to perform and went on to the next destination.

932
00:57:58.887 --> 00:58:09.302
But I would think that they stayed somewhere here in Lake Charles, because we had no facilities over there for them to stay overnight.

933
00:58:09.302 --> 00:58:17.981
There was several . . . During those times, I guess they would have had to stay at the black hotels over here,

934
00:58:17.981 --> 00:58:25.358
because I don't think they . . . during those times they weren't allowed to stay in the white hotels.

935
00:58:25.358 --> 00:58:31.000
And there were several black hotels in the area during . . . back then.

936
00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:33.075
<v TOWNER>Did [Balls?] have one?

937
00:58:33.075 --> 00:58:33.594
<v AMBROSE>[Balls?] . . .

938
00:58:33.594 --> 00:58:34.632
<v TOWNER>A hotel?

939
00:58:34.632 --> 00:58:35.670
<v AMBROSE>. . . did not.

940
00:58:35.670 --> 00:58:38.264
<v TOWNER>They didn't have a hotel?

941
00:58:38.264 --> 00:58:40.340
<v AMBROSE>[Disagrees] No, they didn't.

942
00:58:40.340 --> 00:58:45.528
They just had the auditorium and the fried chicken place.

943
00:58:45.528 --> 00:58:55.387
But they had . . . Now they may have stayed at [Asanto's?], which was a white-owned motel up on Broad Street.

944
00:58:55.387 --> 00:59:03.170
Then they had Valery's Motel down on Enterprise by the freeway where Popeye's is now.

945
00:59:03.170 --> 00:59:06.802
Those are the ones that I remember.

946
00:59:06.802 --> 00:59:11.991
But that was in the '60s after I was married.

947
00:59:11.991 --> 00:59:18.736
So whether they stayed at those back during that time, I don't know.

948
00:59:18.736 --> 00:59:26.000
I'm trying to think of some of the other artists that we had.

949
00:59:26.000 --> 00:59:28.250
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mr. Valery ever put out posters?

950
00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:28.893
<v AMBROSE>Oh, yeah.

951
00:59:28.893 --> 00:59:29.857
They had posters.

952
00:59:29.857 --> 00:59:31.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was the artist?

953
00:59:31.143 --> 00:59:33.714
<v AMBROSE>For all the artists they would have posters.

954
00:59:33.714 --> 00:59:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who drew them?

955
00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:39.473
<v AMBROSE>They were made by some company here in Lake Charles or Sulphur.

956
00:59:39.473 --> 00:59:42.455
They weren't hand drawn that I can remember.

957
00:59:42.455 --> 00:59:49.164
They had a lot of companies that did posters, like for school activities and that kind of stuff.

958
00:59:49.164 --> 00:59:51.773
But they were not . . . that I remember.

959
00:59:51.773 --> 00:59:55.500
Now maybe there were some that I didn't know about,

960
00:59:55.500 --> 01:00:03.327
but I think . . . They all look like they were . . . Did you ever see any of those posters a long time ago?

961
01:00:03.327 --> 01:00:04.818
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees] I don't remember.

962
01:00:04.818 --> 01:00:06.682
<v AMBROSE>They were just regular posters.

963
01:00:06.682 --> 01:00:09.664
Mostly it was word of mouth and radio.

964
01:00:09.664 --> 01:00:13.391
I don’t really know how many posters he put out.

965
01:00:13.391 --> 01:00:16.000
I just remember seeing a few.

966
01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:20.375
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any local musicians?

967
01:00:20.375 --> 01:00:21.250
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

968
01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:26.500
We had Cookie and the Cupcakes.

969
01:00:26.500 --> 01:00:30.000
We had [Little Alfred?].

970
01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:40.500
We had . . . the one [Don't Mess with my Tutu?] [Black and Sydney?].

971
01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:45.750
<v TOWNER>Who's the one . . . Robert [Body's?] band?

972
01:00:45.750 --> 01:00:50.125
They have something every September.

973
01:00:50.125 --> 01:00:51.875
<v AMBROSE>[Buzu Chavis?]

974
01:00:51.875 --> 01:00:52.750
<v TOWNER>Yes.

975
01:00:52.750 --> 01:00:56.250
<v AMBROSE>He was more Zydeco.

976
01:00:56.250 --> 01:00:58.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

977
01:00:58.000 --> 01:00:59.684
<v ARSENEAULT>He was from Mossville?

978
01:00:59.684 --> 01:01:02.211
<v AMBROSE>No. He was from Lake Charles.

979
01:01:02.211 --> 01:01:06.000
These are all that I'm naming from Lake Charles.

980
01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:08.526
I don't remember any bands in . . .

981
01:01:08.526 --> 01:01:09.368
<v TOWNER>In Mossville?

982
01:01:09.368 --> 01:01:10.211
<v AMBROSE>. . . in Mossville.

983
01:01:10.211 --> 01:01:11.474
We had musicians.

984
01:01:11.474 --> 01:01:11.895
<v TOWNER>Yes.

985
01:01:11.895 --> 01:01:14.000
<v AMBROSE>But I don't remember . . .

986
01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:15.027
<v TOWNER>No bands.

987
01:01:15.027 --> 01:01:17.595
<v AMBROSE>And we had gospel singers.

988
01:01:17.595 --> 01:01:19.649
We had gospel quartets.

989
01:01:19.649 --> 01:01:24.784
But I don't remember any bands that really played anywhere.

990
01:01:24.784 --> 01:01:27.351
<v TOWNER>Lee and Uncle [Hooker?] [laughs]

991
01:01:27.351 --> 01:01:28.892
<v AMBROSE>They played music?

992
01:01:28.892 --> 01:01:31.973
See, that was before my time.

993
01:01:31.973 --> 01:01:33.000
<v TOWNER>Oh.

994
01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:34.600
<v AMBROSE>That was Lee [Verdine?]?

995
01:01:34.600 --> 01:01:35.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

996
01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:35.400
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

997
01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:37.000
And Hooker was Williams?

998
01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:38.200
Last name Williams?

999
01:01:38.200 --> 01:01:38.600
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1000
01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:41.000
<v AMBROSE>So what did they play?

1001
01:01:41.000 --> 01:01:41.500
<v TOWNER>Guitar.

1002
01:01:41.500 --> 01:01:43.000
<v AMBROSE>Both of them?

1003
01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:43.500
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1004
01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:47.000
<v AMBROSE>So did they play in the clubs?

1005
01:01:47.000 --> 01:01:48.000
<v TOWNER>No.

1006
01:01:48.000 --> 01:01:49.306
<v AMBROSE>Where'd they play?

1007
01:01:49.306 --> 01:01:51.484
<v TOWNER>Wherever they got drunk at.

1008
01:01:51.484 --> 01:01:51.919
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1009
01:01:51.919 --> 01:01:54.968
I know we had a lot of . . .

1010
01:01:54.968 --> 01:01:58.887
<v TOWNER>Wherever they got drunk, that's where they played. [laughs]

1011
01:01:58.887 --> 01:02:04.548
<v AMBROSE>I know we had a lot of musicians, especially people who play piano.

1012
01:02:04.548 --> 01:02:08.032
But most of them played in the churches.

1013
01:02:08.032 --> 01:02:10.645
I didn't know we had any . . .

1014
01:02:10.645 --> 01:02:11.081
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1015
01:02:11.081 --> 01:02:11.952
<v AMBROSE>. . . R&amp;B aficionados.

1016
01:02:11.952 --> 01:02:12.387
Okay.

1017
01:02:12.387 --> 01:02:15.000
What was Hooker's first name?

1018
01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:15.437
<v TOWNER>James.

1019
01:02:15.437 --> 01:02:16.310
<v AMBROSE>James Hooker.

1020
01:02:16.310 --> 01:02:17.183
<v TOWNER>James Williams.

1021
01:02:17.183 --> 01:02:19.803
<v AMBROSE>James Hooker Williams and Lee [Verdine?].

1022
01:02:19.803 --> 01:02:26.789
Well let me see if I can think of something else . . . somebody else who played music.

1023
01:02:26.789 --> 01:02:30.282
Garfield [Verdine?], he was one of my contemporaries.

1024
01:02:30.282 --> 01:02:34.211
He was a year or two older than me.

1025
01:02:34.211 --> 01:02:36.831
He was in our school band.

1026
01:02:36.831 --> 01:02:40.761
He later on went to play professional . . . music professionally.

1027
01:02:40.761 --> 01:02:42.507
He was a pianist.

1028
01:02:42.507 --> 01:02:44.254
<v TOWNER>Did you get Zoe?

1029
01:02:44.254 --> 01:02:46.389
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Yes.

1030
01:02:46.389 --> 01:02:48.722
She said she about to come.

1031
01:02:48.722 --> 01:02:51.444
<v AMBROSE>And he played with some renowned artist.

1032
01:02:51.444 --> 01:02:55.333
I'd probably have to ask Joanne who he played with.

1033
01:02:55.333 --> 01:02:57.667
But he played with some professionals.

1034
01:02:57.667 --> 01:03:03.111
He was in California, and he came back here and stayed for a while.

1035
01:03:03.111 --> 01:03:11.278
He would leave and go and play with the . . . whatever the group was, and he'd come back home before he died.

1036
01:03:11.278 --> 01:03:14.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Who was that?

1037
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:14.667
<v AMBROSE>Garfield [Verdine?].

1038
01:03:14.667 --> 01:03:16.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Who?

1039
01:03:16.000 --> 01:03:16.667
<v AMBROSE>Garfield [Verdine?].

1040
01:03:16.667 --> 01:03:19.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Who was he?

1041
01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:21.640
<v AMBROSE>Somebody I went to school with.

1042
01:03:21.640 --> 01:03:23.400
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Oh.

1043
01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:26.040
<v AMBROSE>And then we had another one.

1044
01:03:26.040 --> 01:03:31.760
I can't remember if he played, but he could have, was Timothy O'Neal.

1045
01:03:31.760 --> 01:03:36.160
He was a very good . . . I think he played saxophone.

1046
01:03:36.160 --> 01:03:43.200
He was a musician, a very good musician—could have played professional--that I went to school with.

1047
01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:48.480
<v ARSENEAULT>So for live music in Mossville, was it just the Paradise Club?

1048
01:03:48.480 --> 01:03:52.000
Or could you go somewhere else, too?

1049
01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:55.854
<v AMBROSE>No. They had . . . I guess all of them had people.

1050
01:03:55.854 --> 01:04:01.250
But most of them had . . . They were the big . . . they had the big names,

1051
01:04:01.250 --> 01:04:05.490
because they were the biggest and they could bring more people.

1052
01:04:05.490 --> 01:04:12.042
But they all had people from Lake Charles, because I think they all had music in them.

1053
01:04:12.042 --> 01:04:14.354
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] I know Low Top did.

1054
01:04:14.354 --> 01:04:14.740
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1055
01:04:14.740 --> 01:04:18.594
And that was owned by the Bates family, Low Top.

1056
01:04:18.594 --> 01:04:25.531
And I don't remember Tisha and her husband, who they had back there . . . how much entertainment they had.

1057
01:04:25.531 --> 01:04:25.917
<v TOWNER>Oh.

1058
01:04:25.917 --> 01:04:29.000
See, I didn't know nothing about it.

1059
01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:29.588
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1060
01:04:29.588 --> 01:04:39.000
I never . . . I was too young to go back there, so I never went in there.

1061
01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:42.529
But we just knew about it.

1062
01:04:42.529 --> 01:04:46.059
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Mom, that somebody in your car?

1063
01:04:46.059 --> 01:04:47.235
<v TOWNER>Somewhere [inside?].

1064
01:04:47.235 --> 01:04:53.118
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>I need to use it to go pick up [Lonnie?].

1065
01:04:53.118 --> 01:04:54.294
They gone?

1066
01:04:54.294 --> 01:04:59.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: No, they're right here.

1067
01:04:59.000 --> 01:05:05.000
<v AMBROSE>I'm trying to think of who else.

1068
01:05:05.000 --> 01:05:07.571
<v ARSENEAULT>What about dancing?

1069
01:05:07.571 --> 01:05:13.571
<v AMBROSE>That was all the places, they danced.

1070
01:05:13.571 --> 01:05:16.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Any special dances?

1071
01:05:16.143 --> 01:05:29.000
<v AMBROSE>All the popular dances: the horse, the pony, the alligator, the slop, bunny hop.

1072
01:05:29.000 --> 01:05:31.077
<v ARSENEAULT>What's the alligator?

1073
01:05:31.077 --> 01:05:33.154
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

1074
01:05:33.154 --> 01:05:38.000
Some real stupid thing they would do.

1075
01:05:38.000 --> 01:05:42.846
<v TOWNER>[laughs] I don't remember that one either.

1076
01:05:42.846 --> 01:05:51.154
<v AMBROSE>It's something they made up that the guys used to do that.

1077
01:05:51.154 --> 01:05:54.615
And they had the slop,

1078
01:05:54.615 --> 01:05:58.769
<c.p>and the bunny hop, the fly.

1079
01:05:58.769 --> 01:06:01.538
<v AMBROSE>[background chatter] She's recording.

1080
01:06:01.538 --> 01:06:05.000
<v UNIDENTIFIED CHILD>Now let me in!

1081
01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:05.682
<v TOWNER>Everett . . .

1082
01:06:05.682 --> 01:06:07.727
<v AMBROSE>Everett . . . she's recording.

1083
01:06:07.727 --> 01:06:13.182
I don't think they want to know about . . .

1084
01:06:13.182 --> 01:06:16.591
<v TOWNER>What she had for lunch.

1085
01:06:16.591 --> 01:06:18.636
<v AMBROSE>Let me think.

1086
01:06:18.636 --> 01:06:21.364
Get off of that.

1087
01:06:21.364 --> 01:06:26.818
I want to get back to the syrup.

1088
01:06:26.818 --> 01:06:30.227
Your uncle did the syrup.

1089
01:06:30.227 --> 01:06:35.000
And did he sell his syrup?

1090
01:06:35.000 --> 01:06:35.429
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1091
01:06:35.429 --> 01:06:38.000
<v AMBROSE>And so where was that at?

1092
01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:38.429
<v TOWNER>Jim?

1093
01:06:38.429 --> 01:06:40.571
<v AMBROSE>Yes, where was that at?

1094
01:06:40.571 --> 01:06:44.000
<v TOWNER>Right there in front of Pete's house.

1095
01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:45.138
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1096
01:06:45.138 --> 01:06:46.276
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1097
01:06:46.276 --> 01:06:48.552
<v AMBROSE>That's [Zian?].

1098
01:06:48.552 --> 01:06:54.241
That's one of her granddaughters.

1099
01:06:54.241 --> 01:06:56.517
That's Brooklyn.

1100
01:06:56.517 --> 01:06:59.931
That's another granddaughter.

1101
01:06:59.931 --> 01:07:11.310
So then . . . so we did the stories of the sawmills.

1102
01:07:11.310 --> 01:07:17.000
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE 2: Grandma!

1103
01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:17.444
<v AMBROSE>Shh!

1104
01:07:17.444 --> 01:07:18.333
She's recording.

1105
01:07:18.333 --> 01:07:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So who worked at the sawmills?

1106
01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:22.778
<v AMBROSE>Everybody in the community.

1107
01:07:22.778 --> 01:07:27.667
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] They had a sawmill up there around Evergreen . . . on Evergreen.

1108
01:07:27.667 --> 01:07:30.333
<v AMBROSE>That's the one I'm talking about.

1109
01:07:30.333 --> 01:07:38.333
In fact, somebody said that they were going to . . . well, this is what I heard some time ago,

1110
01:07:38.333 --> 01:07:48.556
that they were going to post it as . . . put a plaque up there in that area to show that was a historical area.

1111
01:07:48.556 --> 01:07:52.111
But now since Sasol has bought it out,

1112
01:07:52.111 --> 01:07:59.667
I don't know if they ever did it or . . . Now I'm sure they won't be doing it.

1113
01:07:59.667 --> 01:08:01.000
<v TOWNER>Probably not.

1114
01:08:01.000 --> 01:08:03.597
<v AMBROSE>Yes, because it's all dug up now.

1115
01:08:03.597 --> 01:08:06.194
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the name of it?

1116
01:08:06.194 --> 01:08:06.935
<v TOWNER>I don't.

1117
01:08:06.935 --> 01:08:13.984
<v AMBROSE>[Disagrees] I just know everybody . . . or a lot of people worked at the sawmill during . . . back during the time.

1118
01:08:13.984 --> 01:08:18.435
And the Brimstone, was that a railroad track that went through there?

1119
01:08:18.435 --> 01:08:20.661
Or was that just a road?

1120
01:08:20.661 --> 01:08:24.000
<v TOWNER>No, I think it was a railroad track.

1121
01:08:24.000 --> 01:08:30.109
<v AMBROSE>Okay, because I don't remember any tracks over there, but I would know we would walk it.

1122
01:08:30.109 --> 01:08:32.266
But I don't remember any tracks.

1123
01:08:32.266 --> 01:08:36.219
<v TOWNER>Yes, I think that was way before our time, I think.

1124
01:08:36.219 --> 01:08:38.375
But yes, it was a track.

1125
01:08:38.375 --> 01:08:40.531
<v AMBROSE>And now they're using it again.

1126
01:08:40.531 --> 01:08:41.250
<v TOWNER>They are?

1127
01:08:41.250 --> 01:08:43.047
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] Sasol is using it.

1128
01:08:43.047 --> 01:08:50.594
I went up there by Aunt Helen's the other day, and I guess it came down back behind the swimming pool,

1129
01:08:50.594 --> 01:08:54.547
down between Aunt Helen and Aunt Rita, all the way down.

1130
01:08:54.547 --> 01:08:57.422
And they had these cross ties on it,

1131
01:08:57.422 --> 01:09:07.125
and they had it all built up and they're moving heavy equipment all along back there so they don't have to come down the Old Spanish Trail;

1132
01:09:07.125 --> 01:09:10.000
they just go out the back way.

1133
01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:10.789
<v TOWNER>Oh, okay.

1134
01:09:10.789 --> 01:09:11.579
Oh, wow.

1135
01:09:11.579 --> 01:09:13.553
<v AMBROSE>So they're using that again.

1136
01:09:13.553 --> 01:09:17.105
And then it went all the way into Sulphur.

1137
01:09:17.105 --> 01:09:20.263
So I don't know how far into Sulphur.

1138
01:09:20.263 --> 01:09:24.211
But I know they have a Brimstone museum in Sulphur.

1139
01:09:24.211 --> 01:09:27.763
So all that's part of coming down through Mossville.

1140
01:09:27.763 --> 01:09:36.053
And I don't know how many people have talked about that, or said anything about it, but it's a part of . . .

1141
01:09:36.053 --> 01:09:37.237
<v DIGILORMO>We went there.

1142
01:09:37.237 --> 01:09:40.000
<v AMBROSE>To the museum, the Brimstone Museum?

1143
01:09:40.000 --> 01:09:40.500
<v DIGILORMO>Yes.

1144
01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:44.500
Someone said they washed clothes in the Brimstone.

1145
01:09:44.500 --> 01:09:47.500
<v AMBROSE>They probably had a hose . . . wash.

1146
01:09:47.500 --> 01:09:48.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1147
01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:50.000
<v AMBROSE>What they call them?

1148
01:09:50.000 --> 01:09:51.000
Dynamite holes?

1149
01:09:51.000 --> 01:09:53.000
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] They did.

1150
01:09:53.000 --> 01:09:54.667
<v AMBROSE>Along the Brimstone?

1151
01:09:54.667 --> 01:09:55.222
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1152
01:09:55.222 --> 01:09:55.778
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1153
01:09:55.778 --> 01:10:01.889
<v TOWNER>That's where Walton and them would slip off and go swimming.

1154
01:10:01.889 --> 01:10:03.000
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1155
01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:06.000
<v TOWNER>And they told them not to.

1156
01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:10.000
<v AMBROSE>And off the Brimstone, the [Vincent’s?] owned property.

1157
01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:15.000
What was . . . [Chester Vincent?], was that the daddy's name, Chester?

1158
01:10:15.000 --> 01:10:15.500
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1159
01:10:15.500 --> 01:10:19.500
<v AMBROSE>This, I learned since I've been an adult.

1160
01:10:19.500 --> 01:10:30.500
But back behind his house, they said that there was a rice field that they used to grow rice and raise cattle,

1161
01:10:30.500 --> 01:10:33.500
and there was a canal or

1162
01:10:33.500 --> 01:10:36.500
<c.p>something that they would go swimming.

1163
01:10:36.500 --> 01:10:39.000
Do you remember that, Marie?

1164
01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:40.000
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees]

1165
01:10:40.000 --> 01:10:43.000
<v AMBROSE>They said that there were canals back there.

1166
01:10:43.000 --> 01:10:43.375
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1167
01:10:43.375 --> 01:10:48.250
<v AMBROSE>But I never knew what was back there, but never had no reason.

1168
01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:55.750
I guess the guys would go back there and see what was going on, but we never went back there.

1169
01:10:55.750 --> 01:10:58.375
So I didn't even know it existed.

1170
01:10:58.375 --> 01:11:02.875
And they said that it was a big, humongous place back there.

1171
01:11:02.875 --> 01:11:07.375
So did you know about it, Marie, about that being back there?

1172
01:11:07.375 --> 01:11:07.750
<v TOWNER>What?

1173
01:11:07.750 --> 01:11:10.000
<v AMBROSE>About the canals and the . . .

1174
01:11:10.000 --> 01:11:12.073
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] I knew about it.

1175
01:11:12.073 --> 01:11:14.561
<v AMBROSE>But you never went back there?

1176
01:11:14.561 --> 01:11:19.537
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees] It was a [along the Houston River?] there somewhere over there.

1177
01:11:19.537 --> 01:11:22.854
You know where the ferry used to be?

1178
01:11:22.854 --> 01:11:23.268
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1179
01:11:23.268 --> 01:11:27.000
<v TOWNER>It was over in that area, I think.

1180
01:11:27.000 --> 01:11:36.156
<v AMBROSE>Oh, they said it was a . . . He had a big . . . lots of property back there.

1181
01:11:36.156 --> 01:11:36.766
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1182
01:11:36.766 --> 01:11:41.039
That was Mister . . . that was the Vincent’s.

1183
01:11:41.039 --> 01:11:41.649
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1184
01:11:41.649 --> 01:11:51.416
<v TOWNER>So that was . . . And they said that he was, I guess, a rancher and rice farmer,

1185
01:11:51.416 --> 01:12:03.013
or he farmed it out to other people, maybe rented it out to other people. [background chatter] She's recording.

1186
01:12:03.013 --> 01:12:06.675
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 3: She knows everything.

1187
01:12:06.675 --> 01:12:07.896
Yes ma’am.

1188
01:12:07.896 --> 01:12:14.000
No. See you later, baby. [ . . . ?] The one and only.

1189
01:12:14.000 --> 01:12:17.167
<v TOWNER>Excuse me. [laughs] I have to . . .

1190
01:12:17.167 --> 01:12:21.917
<c.p>UNIDENTIFIED MALE 3: [ . . . ?] I told you all about Mossville.

1191
01:12:21.917 --> 01:12:25.083
<v AMBROSE>And he don't know nothing. [laughs]

1192
01:12:25.083 --> 01:12:25.611
<v TOWNER>Nothing.

1193
01:12:25.611 --> 01:12:27.194
Absolutely nothing. [laughs]

1194
01:12:27.194 --> 01:12:29.833
<v ARSENEAULT>So there were dynamite holes?

1195
01:12:29.833 --> 01:12:33.000
Sorry, I missed that part.

1196
01:12:33.000 --> 01:12:34.875
<v AMBROSE>Yes, there were dynamite holes.

1197
01:12:34.875 --> 01:12:38.250
And the women would use to wash in them.

1198
01:12:38.250 --> 01:12:40.125
That was before my time.

1199
01:12:40.125 --> 01:12:45.000
<v TOWNER>Yes, Paw-Paw used to haul water by the barrel from the dynamite holes.

1200
01:12:45.000 --> 01:12:45.750
<v AMBROSE>For what?

1201
01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:46.500
<v TOWNER>To wash.

1202
01:12:46.500 --> 01:12:47.250
<v AMBROSE>To wash?

1203
01:12:47.250 --> 01:12:48.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1204
01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:48.339
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1205
01:12:48.339 --> 01:12:52.750
<v TOWNER>For the women that didn't want to go there and get . . . wash at . . .

1206
01:12:52.750 --> 01:12:53.089
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1207
01:12:53.089 --> 01:12:54.107
<v TOWNER>He'd haul water.

1208
01:12:54.107 --> 01:12:55.804
I think about fifteen cents.

1209
01:12:55.804 --> 01:12:58.518
<v AMBROSE>Well that's part of history I didn't know.

1210
01:12:58.518 --> 01:13:06.321
So he just would get those big barrels and put water on the back of the truck . . . on the back of the wagon . . .

1211
01:13:06.321 --> 01:13:07.000
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1212
01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:09.578
<v AMBROSE>. . . and just take it to them?

1213
01:13:09.578 --> 01:13:18.172
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] He had something rigged up there so he could dip that bucket . . . would go down, get the water up . . .

1214
01:13:18.172 --> 01:13:20.320
<v AMBROSE>Put it in the barrel.

1215
01:13:20.320 --> 01:13:20.750
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1216
01:13:20.750 --> 01:13:29.344
<v AMBROSE>So that's another . . . We had a lot of people who had a lot . . . They were geniuses in figuring things out.

1217
01:13:29.344 --> 01:13:37.078
Now who would have thought to sell water out of the dynamite hole for people to wash at?

1218
01:13:37.078 --> 01:13:38.367
<v TOWNER>Yes, sure did.

1219
01:13:38.367 --> 01:13:39.227
I forgot.

1220
01:13:39.227 --> 01:13:44.383
Paw-Paw had a truck too at one time, with the wooden wheels.

1221
01:13:44.383 --> 01:13:48.680
And it sat out front of the house for years.

1222
01:13:48.680 --> 01:13:49.539
Sure did.

1223
01:13:49.539 --> 01:13:51.688
Yes, he had a truck.

1224
01:13:51.688 --> 01:13:59.422
So he did have a truck for him. [laughs] He stepped up from a wagon to a truck.

1225
01:13:59.422 --> 01:14:02.000
He had a truck . . . had . . .

1226
01:14:02.000 --> 01:14:03.385
<v AMBROSE>Wood wheels.

1227
01:14:03.385 --> 01:14:06.154
<v TOWNER>. . . wooden wheels on it.

1228
01:14:06.154 --> 01:14:07.538
Sure did.

1229
01:14:07.538 --> 01:14:15.154
And it sat out in front of their house for years.

1230
01:14:15.154 --> 01:14:17.231
<v AMBROSE>Isn't that something!

1231
01:14:17.231 --> 01:14:21.385
That was before my time, too.

1232
01:14:21.385 --> 01:14:22.077
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1233
01:14:22.077 --> 01:14:23.462
<v AMBROSE>That's interesting.

1234
01:14:23.462 --> 01:14:24.846
Very interesting.

1235
01:14:24.846 --> 01:14:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did the sawmill close?

1236
01:14:29.000 --> 01:14:29.755
<v AMBROSE>Oh, Lord.

1237
01:14:29.755 --> 01:14:32.396
Maybe the 1920s or something like that.

1238
01:14:32.396 --> 01:14:35.792
Or was it open when you were a kid?

1239
01:14:35.792 --> 01:14:37.679
<v TOWNER>No. I wasn't real young.

1240
01:14:37.679 --> 01:14:39.566
So that had to be . . .

1241
01:14:39.566 --> 01:14:40.698
<v AMBROSE>In the '40s.

1242
01:14:40.698 --> 01:14:42.208
<v TOWNER>. . . in the '40s, yes.

1243
01:14:42.208 --> 01:14:44.472
I'd say closed in the mid-'40s.

1244
01:14:44.472 --> 01:14:48.245
<v AMBROSE>Yes, because it . . . when I came up it wasn't there.

1245
01:14:48.245 --> 01:14:49.000
<v TOWNER>No.

1246
01:14:49.000 --> 01:14:53.195
<v AMBROSE>I just knew about it, but never saw it.

1247
01:14:53.195 --> 01:14:56.458
<v TOWNER>I think Mr. Hardy Edwards worked there.

1248
01:14:56.458 --> 01:14:56.924
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1249
01:14:56.924 --> 01:15:02.518
That might be something . . . I'm going to have to ask Sister . . . Sunny,

1250
01:15:02.518 --> 01:15:08.578
maybe they can give us some information on their side of the family.

1251
01:15:08.578 --> 01:15:10.442
That's the Edwards family.

1252
01:15:10.442 --> 01:15:12.307
<v ARSENEAULT>Why did it close?

1253
01:15:12.307 --> 01:15:14.637
<v AMBROSE>They didn't need it anymore.

1254
01:15:14.637 --> 01:15:21.163
You had your lumber companies coming in where you didn't need the small sawmills.

1255
01:15:21.163 --> 01:15:31.884
Like Daddy used to say, some people had small sawmills like in their yards that they could cut trees down on their property,

1256
01:15:31.884 --> 01:15:38.876
and then they would mill the . . . But they didn't have a need for the sawmills,

1257
01:15:38.876 --> 01:15:43.538
because they'd just go buy their lumber at a lumberyard.

1258
01:15:43.538 --> 01:15:46.335
So you know how things go.

1259
01:15:46.335 --> 01:15:51.462
New things come in, and the old things are over with.

1260
01:15:51.462 --> 01:15:59.386
Because it was like Daddy said, a long time ago there were was a lot of farming,

1261
01:15:59.386 --> 01:16:04.514
a lot of rice farms and cattle ranches in this area,

1262
01:16:04.514 --> 01:16:13.371
and probably even up to the time maybe when we were growing up in the '40s and the '50s,

1263
01:16:13.371 --> 01:16:23.159
everybody didn’t [?], because it seemed like there was some rice being farmed down on the Old Spanish Trail going towards Sulphur.

1264
01:16:23.159 --> 01:16:31.550
Well then, all of a sudden, that all went away and people started selling their property for houses.

1265
01:16:31.550 --> 01:16:35.279
People started building homes and what have you.

1266
01:16:35.279 --> 01:16:37.610
So that was another thing.

1267
01:16:37.610 --> 01:16:44.135
And I don't know really where we have rice farms today left around here.

1268
01:16:44.135 --> 01:16:46.000
I don't know.

1269
01:16:46.000 --> 01:16:48.736
<v TOWNER>I don't think it's around here.

1270
01:16:48.736 --> 01:16:53.751
<v AMBROSE>Because back in Sulphur, south of Sulphur, there were rice fields.

1271
01:16:53.751 --> 01:16:57.399
There were rice fields between here and Vinton.

1272
01:16:57.399 --> 01:17:00.591
They were just all over the place.

1273
01:17:00.591 --> 01:17:07.430
Industry came in and people stopped working with farms and started working at the plants.

1274
01:17:07.430 --> 01:17:10.622
So there was no demand for it.

1275
01:17:10.622 --> 01:17:18.373
When they had it east of us, they had lots of land, and lots of rice farmers,

1276
01:17:18.373 --> 01:17:25.212
lots of sugar cane around Opelousas [Louisiana], and Jennings [Louisiana], and all around that area.

1277
01:17:25.212 --> 01:17:32.052
So they went to industry down here so the farming just kind of went away.

1278
01:17:32.052 --> 01:17:42.539
And I don't know if the plants started coming in . . . And I know the plant that I worked at came in 1932, Olin.

1279
01:17:42.539 --> 01:17:45.275
<v ARSENEAULT>And that was the first one?

1280
01:17:45.275 --> 01:17:49.834
<v AMBROSE>I think that was the first plant in the area.

1281
01:17:49.834 --> 01:17:54.850
And then, of course, you had your Cities Service and your . . .

1282
01:17:54.850 --> 01:17:55.306
<v TOWNER>Firestone.

1283
01:17:55.306 --> 01:17:58.497
<v AMBROSE>. . . Firestone, PPG, Hercules, Conoco, all those plants.

1284
01:17:58.497 --> 01:18:04.425
It was just lots of plants, and people started working in the plants.

1285
01:18:04.425 --> 01:18:11.264
Your laborers that would probably have worked on the farms started going into the plants.

1286
01:18:11.264 --> 01:18:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did Mossville get water?

1287
01:18:14.000 --> 01:18:15.032
<v AMBROSE>Public water?

1288
01:18:15.032 --> 01:18:16.581
<v TOWNER>After I left.

1289
01:18:16.581 --> 01:18:22.774
<v AMBROSE>It had to be, what, in the '70s maybe, or the '80s?

1290
01:18:22.774 --> 01:18:32.581
You probably need to talk to somebody that's . . . Butch, or [Duhon?], the girl that works at the water company.

1291
01:18:32.581 --> 01:18:37.226
They can tell you exactly when it came in.

1292
01:18:37.226 --> 01:18:40.323
<v TOWNER>I don't think they're there anymore.

1293
01:18:40.323 --> 01:18:41.871
<v AMBROSE>Where are they?

1294
01:18:41.871 --> 01:18:42.903
Water's gone?

1295
01:18:42.903 --> 01:18:46.000
They don't have water anymore?

1296
01:18:46.000 --> 01:18:47.565
<v TOWNER>Yes, they have water.

1297
01:18:47.565 --> 01:18:49.913
<v AMBROSE>The Duhon girl's not there anymore?

1298
01:18:49.913 --> 01:18:52.261
<v TOWNER>I think somebody took it over.

1299
01:18:52.261 --> 01:18:53.043
<v AMBROSE>Oh, okay.

1300
01:18:53.043 --> 01:18:53.826
Well then . . .

1301
01:18:53.826 --> 01:18:55.000
<v TOWNER>They retired.

1302
01:18:55.000 --> 01:18:55.803
<v AMBROSE>Oh really?

1303
01:18:55.803 --> 01:19:01.426
Well I'll have to try to find it out for you, because I was

1304
01:19:01.426 --> 01:19:04.639
<c.p>already over here when the water came in.

1305
01:19:04.639 --> 01:19:05.844
<v TOWNER>We had wells.

1306
01:19:05.844 --> 01:19:07.852
<v AMBROSE>Yes, we always had wells.

1307
01:19:07.852 --> 01:19:14.279
My grandfather had our well at our house was five hundred . . . over five hundred feet deep,

1308
01:19:14.279 --> 01:19:17.893
and we had sweet, sweet water, really good water.

1309
01:19:17.893 --> 01:19:20.303
We still have the well there.

1310
01:19:20.303 --> 01:19:23.918
I don't know . . . probably have to drop more pipes,

1311
01:19:23.918 --> 01:19:31.148
because I'm sure all the plants using all the water, they use a water level that's probably dropped.

1312
01:19:31.148 --> 01:19:35.566
But everybody had their own wells when I was growing up.

1313
01:19:35.566 --> 01:19:41.992
And then the sewage, that just came in in recent years, the last couple of years.

1314
01:19:41.992 --> 01:19:44.000
<v TOWNER>Couple of years, yes.

1315
01:19:44.000 --> 01:19:48.825
<v AMBROSE>And it stopped down by . . . where did it stop?

1316
01:19:48.825 --> 01:19:50.433
At the church.

1317
01:19:50.433 --> 01:19:52.041
Because Sasol started . . .

1318
01:19:52.041 --> 01:19:53.113
<v ARSENEAULT>What church?

1319
01:19:53.113 --> 01:19:54.186
<v AMBROSE>Mount Zion.

1320
01:19:54.186 --> 01:20:02.763
Sasol started buying up everything, and it was supposed to have gone all the way through.

1321
01:20:02.763 --> 01:20:10.268
But when Sasol started buying up everything, they stopped it right at Mount Zion.

1322
01:20:10.268 --> 01:20:15.093
Because you didn't get . . . y'all didn't get it either?

1323
01:20:15.093 --> 01:20:15.629
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees]

1324
01:20:15.629 --> 01:20:20.454
<v AMBROSE>Mount Zion, because they stopped just before Mount Zion.

1325
01:20:20.454 --> 01:20:24.742
So nobody from Mount Zion down has sewage.

1326
01:20:24.742 --> 01:20:29.567
And I think they were on Westlake . . . Westlake Sewage.

1327
01:20:29.567 --> 01:20:32.784
And I think Westlake's water too.

1328
01:20:32.784 --> 01:20:36.000
Or was it our water?

1329
01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:36.517
<v TOWNER>Beauregard?

1330
01:20:36.517 --> 01:20:38.586
<v AMBROSE>No, Beauregard is DeRidder.

1331
01:20:38.586 --> 01:20:41.172
The water was on Mossville.

1332
01:20:41.172 --> 01:20:42.724
<v TOWNER>The what now?

1333
01:20:42.724 --> 01:20:43.759
<v AMBROSE>The water.

1334
01:20:43.759 --> 01:20:46.862
Was it tied in with Westlake?

1335
01:20:46.862 --> 01:20:48.414
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees] They had . . .

1336
01:20:48.414 --> 01:20:51.000
<v AMBROSE>They had their own [?].

1337
01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:51.441
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1338
01:20:51.441 --> 01:20:54.088
<v AMBROSE>Their own power plant . . . water plant.

1339
01:20:54.088 --> 01:20:59.824
So I guess it was just the sewage, then, that came from Westlake.

1340
01:20:59.824 --> 01:21:03.353
<v TOWNER>But I think Beauregard took it over now.

1341
01:21:03.353 --> 01:21:03.794
<v AMBROSE>Oh.

1342
01:21:03.794 --> 01:21:04.676
<v TOWNER>Beauregard is . . .

1343
01:21:04.676 --> 01:21:06.000
<v AMBROSE>Moss Bluff.

1344
01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:08.899
<v TOWNER>Yes, Moss Bluff had Beauregard.

1345
01:21:08.899 --> 01:21:09.478
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1346
01:21:09.478 --> 01:21:17.594
Because I know Mossville has Beauregard Electric, and then they have Beauregard Water too.

1347
01:21:17.594 --> 01:21:19.333
I don't know.

1348
01:21:19.333 --> 01:21:31.507
There was something else I was thinking about . . . the next time I saw you I was going to tell you about.

1349
01:21:31.507 --> 01:21:35.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Did I ask you about integration already?

1350
01:21:35.565 --> 01:21:39.043
Did we already talk about that?

1351
01:21:39.043 --> 01:21:41.362
<v AMBROSE>We touched on it.

1352
01:21:41.362 --> 01:21:44.261
<v TOWNER>Would you like a Coke?

1353
01:21:44.261 --> 01:21:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm good.

1354
01:21:46.000 --> 01:21:46.778
<v TOWNER>Oh, okay.

1355
01:21:46.778 --> 01:21:49.889
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you want to get something to drink?

1356
01:21:49.889 --> 01:21:50.278
<v TOWNER>No.

1357
01:21:50.278 --> 01:21:51.833
<v ARSENEAULT>We can unclip you.

1358
01:21:51.833 --> 01:21:56.500
<v AMBROSE>I think the last class that went to Mossville was '68, '69.

1359
01:21:56.500 --> 01:22:00.389
We didn't have a lot of problems when integration started.

1360
01:22:00.389 --> 01:22:03.111
Lake Charles had a lot of problems.

1361
01:22:03.111 --> 01:22:05.833
Moss Bluff had a lot of problems.

1362
01:22:05.833 --> 01:22:13.222
The kids who went to . . . after I had graduated from high school that went to integrated Westlake and Sulphur,

1363
01:22:13.222 --> 01:22:15.556
they had a lot of problems.

1364
01:22:15.556 --> 01:22:18.667
Westlake, I think, had more problems than Sulphur.

1365
01:22:18.667 --> 01:22:25.278
Westlake had a lot of . . . Well, I guess I know more kids that went to integrated Westlake.

1366
01:22:25.278 --> 01:22:27.611
They had a lot of problems.

1367
01:22:27.611 --> 01:22:29.167
Of course our kids [ . . . ?].

1368
01:22:29.167 --> 01:22:32.278
They had the Delafosses and Edward Julia's bunch.

1369
01:22:32.278 --> 01:22:39.278
And the kids played football, so they weren't going to be mistreated and just be there for football.

1370
01:22:39.278 --> 01:22:42.000
But they had lots of problems.

1371
01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:43.724
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any?

1372
01:22:43.724 --> 01:22:49.760
<v AMBROSE>But as far as the community, we didn't have crosses burned that I remember.

1373
01:22:49.760 --> 01:22:50.622
<v TOWNER>Me either.

1374
01:22:50.622 --> 01:22:53.209
<v AMBROSE>I don't remember having crosses burned.

1375
01:22:53.209 --> 01:22:57.089
Lake Charles had an awful lot of crosses burned.

1376
01:22:57.089 --> 01:22:59.676
Moss Bluff had homes burned down.

1377
01:22:59.676 --> 01:23:08.729
But I guess because we were in a black . . . predominately black community, had our own school, we didn't have that problem.

1378
01:23:08.729 --> 01:23:17.351
It was when our kids went out to the schools in the white neighborhoods, then, of course they ran into

1379
01:23:17.351 --> 01:23:21.662
<c.p>problems: a lot of fighting, a lot of name calling.

1380
01:23:21.662 --> 01:23:29.422
Then, of course, some of the parents got involved that . . . with their children who were having the problems.

1381
01:23:29.422 --> 01:23:34.596
And it was some hard times for those children, for those kids.

1382
01:23:34.596 --> 01:23:37.613
But they all made it through it.

1383
01:23:37.613 --> 01:23:45.804
I don't know how long it lasted because I wasn't in school at that time; I was already gone.

1384
01:23:45.804 --> 01:23:49.253
But for several years there were major problems.

1385
01:23:49.253 --> 01:23:56.582
And like I said, I know more . . . And Westlake High, I'm sure Sulphur had the same problem,

1386
01:23:56.582 --> 01:24:06.929
but I just don't know that many kids that went to Sulphur, because half of Mossville went to Westlake and half went to Sulphur.

1387
01:24:06.929 --> 01:24:13.396
And the more . . . I guess the more demonstrative ones, the louder ones, went to Westlake.

1388
01:24:13.396 --> 01:24:19.000
And by the time you got on our end of the highway . . .

1389
01:24:19.000 --> 01:24:21.123
<v TOWNER>They'd calm down some.

1390
01:24:21.123 --> 01:24:23.247
<v AMBROSE>Yes, we had that.

1391
01:24:23.247 --> 01:24:26.963
We weren't that same kind of people.

1392
01:24:26.963 --> 01:24:46.074
Because I'm just trying to think now who were the kids that went to . . . On our end of the road would have been like Aunt Mildred’s bunch . . . I don't know if Carrie went to Sulphur High.

1393
01:24:46.074 --> 01:24:49.790
The LaTours, which they fell right in.

1394
01:24:49.790 --> 01:24:51.914
The [Clofuses?] had children.

1395
01:24:51.914 --> 01:24:55.630
<v TOWNER>The [Clofuses?] been out here that long?

1396
01:24:55.630 --> 01:25:00.407
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] They were here, I think, before I married.

1397
01:25:00.407 --> 01:25:02.000
<v TOWNER>Oh, really?

1398
01:25:02.000 --> 01:25:04.035
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] Those older kids.

1399
01:25:04.035 --> 01:25:08.105
I don't know any of the younger ones.

1400
01:25:08.105 --> 01:25:11.667
But like the . . . Johnny and that girl.

1401
01:25:11.667 --> 01:25:14.211
I can't remember her name.

1402
01:25:14.211 --> 01:25:20.316
But they were . . . I think they were in Mossville before I married.

1403
01:25:20.316 --> 01:25:27.947
The [Moreau’s?] . . . Now whether there were any kids that went to Sulphur, I don't know.

1404
01:25:27.947 --> 01:25:32.526
That Evergreen Road bunch, they probably went to Westlake.

1405
01:25:32.526 --> 01:25:40.667
And there may have been some . . . I don't know if they split them up or not,

1406
01:25:40.667 --> 01:25:46.263
some going to Sulphur and some of them going to Westlake.

1407
01:25:46.263 --> 01:25:50.842
But I think they might have went to Westlake.

1408
01:25:50.842 --> 01:25:51.860
What else?

1409
01:25:51.860 --> 01:25:54.912
The Edward’s, the Jordan’s, the Rougeau’s.

1410
01:25:54.912 --> 01:25:56.439
Is it Rougeau’s?

1411
01:25:56.439 --> 01:25:57.456
Yes, Rougeau’s.

1412
01:25:57.456 --> 01:26:03.561
The [DeJean’s?], the Guillory’s, all of that would have went to Sulphur.

1413
01:26:03.561 --> 01:26:07.632
I'm trying to think of some other families.

1414
01:26:07.632 --> 01:26:13.737
Back there behind Queensboro, that would have been the [Eagan’s?], the [Noels?].

1415
01:26:13.737 --> 01:26:19.333
And they may . . . Like I say, they may have had trouble,

1416
01:26:19.333 --> 01:26:26.456
but you didn't hear as much from them as you did the Westlake bunch.

1417
01:26:26.456 --> 01:26:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about voter registration?

1418
01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:32.637
<v AMBROSE>That was all before I graduated from high school.

1419
01:26:32.637 --> 01:26:35.062
Everybody was voting who could vote.

1420
01:26:35.062 --> 01:26:43.952
My mother and them used to canvas the areas to make sure everybody would get out and register to vote and vote.

1421
01:26:43.952 --> 01:26:50.014
And then, of course, we voted at Mossville, so we didn't have that outside interference.

1422
01:26:50.014 --> 01:26:52.842
We were just a contained little community.

1423
01:26:52.842 --> 01:26:57.692
We didn't know a lot about what we read in the paper.

1424
01:26:57.692 --> 01:27:04.966
But as far as people coming down through Mossville causing trouble, I don't ever remember any of that.

1425
01:27:04.966 --> 01:27:12.240
So we were kind of a self-contained little community that nobody really gave us a lot of trouble.

1426
01:27:12.240 --> 01:27:15.877
I can figure our trouble was Ms. Vincent's grandkids.

1427
01:27:15.877 --> 01:27:16.281
<v TOWNER>Who?

1428
01:27:16.281 --> 01:27:17.089
Ms. Vincent?

1429
01:27:17.089 --> 01:27:19.918
<v AMBROSE>Ms. Vincent's grandkids: [Cici?] and that bunch.

1430
01:27:19.918 --> 01:27:28.000
That was only because we would eavesdrop on her . . . she and her boyfriend when they were on the phone.

1431
01:27:28.000 --> 01:27:30.735
<v TOWNER>So you wasn't supposed to be eavesdropping [laughs].

1432
01:27:30.735 --> 01:27:31.419
<v AMBROSE>I know.

1433
01:27:31.419 --> 01:27:34.839
And Mom [would have seen?], she would have killed us.

1434
01:27:34.839 --> 01:27:36.890
But [Sissy?] was older than us,

1435
01:27:36.890 --> 01:27:43.387
and her mother . . . her grandmother was the only white person . . . Well we had two white ladies in our community:

1436
01:27:43.387 --> 01:27:44.755
Ms. Vincent and [Mon?].

1437
01:27:44.755 --> 01:27:47.490
I don't even remember what [Mon's?] name was.

1438
01:27:47.490 --> 01:27:50.910
And Ms. Vincent, I don't remember her having a husband.

1439
01:27:50.910 --> 01:27:56.039
But she had sons and they were all friends of my uncle's, my mother's brothers.

1440
01:27:56.039 --> 01:27:58.090
So we didn't know any difference.

1441
01:27:58.090 --> 01:27:59.800
They were like . . . [ . . .?] [and booing?].

1442
01:27:59.800 --> 01:28:05.955
And Ms. [Sissy?] was older, so she was already married and lived right down the road from us.

1443
01:28:05.955 --> 01:28:09.374
And I can't remember what her older daughter was named.

1444
01:28:09.374 --> 01:28:12.452
She had two girls . . . two girls and two boys.

1445
01:28:12.452 --> 01:28:14.503
Anyway, we didn't know any difference.

1446
01:28:14.503 --> 01:28:18.948
Ms. Vincent was Ms. Vincent, and Edwin was Edwin, and [Booing?] was [Booing?].

1447
01:28:18.948 --> 01:28:20.658
And they were like family.

1448
01:28:20.658 --> 01:28:24.761
We knew who Ms. Sissy was . . . I mean Ms. Sissy's momma was,

1449
01:28:24.761 --> 01:28:29.206
and they lived right down where we had to go the grocery store.

1450
01:28:29.206 --> 01:28:33.994
We had a little grocery store right . . . a block or so, two blocks away.

1451
01:28:33.994 --> 01:28:42.200
And one day we were on the phone and we were listening to [Sissy?], and of course we had to comment on her conversation.

1452
01:28:42.200 --> 01:28:48.697
She said . . . she called us "little chocolate boogers," that we better get off the phone listening to her conversation.

1453
01:28:48.697 --> 01:28:57.245
And we [convinced?] to tell her, "Now if you just want to meet and fight, then we will meet you and we'll have it out."

1454
01:28:57.245 --> 01:29:01.348
And her boyfriend told her, "You better not call them 'little chocolate

1455
01:29:01.348 --> 01:29:07.161
<c.p>boogers', and you better not go down there because they may just chase you right back home."

1456
01:29:07.161 --> 01:29:14.000
So we get the broom and the mop and Joyce and Dorothy . . . Joyce and somebody else across the street . . .

1457
01:29:14.000 --> 01:29:16.060
<v TOWNER>It was a bunch of y'all?

1458
01:29:16.060 --> 01:29:16.746
<v AMBROSE>Oh yeah.

1459
01:29:16.746 --> 01:29:19.836
We was going to go whip up on [Sissy?].

1460
01:29:19.836 --> 01:29:21.896
Well [Sissy?] never showed up [laughs].

1461
01:29:21.896 --> 01:29:23.955
So we didn't get in trouble.

1462
01:29:23.955 --> 01:29:26.358
But that was just a neighborhood thing.

1463
01:29:26.358 --> 01:29:28.761
<v TOWNER>Oh, if [Martin?] would have known that.

1464
01:29:28.761 --> 01:29:30.134
<v AMBROSE>We would have died.

1465
01:29:30.134 --> 01:29:31.851
We would have been dead.

1466
01:29:31.851 --> 01:29:34.597
I wouldn't be here telling that story [laughs].

1467
01:29:34.597 --> 01:29:35.627
<v TOWNER>A little gang.

1468
01:29:35.627 --> 01:29:37.000
Oh my goodness.

1469
01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:37.738
<v AMBROSE>Oh, yeah.

1470
01:29:37.738 --> 01:29:44.006
We got our mop and our broom and we was going to go take care of [Sissy?].

1471
01:29:44.006 --> 01:29:44.375
Lord.

1472
01:29:44.375 --> 01:29:48.062
It didn't go no further than the two of us.

1473
01:29:48.062 --> 01:29:53.225
We didn't see her coming, so we just turned around and went back home.

1474
01:29:53.225 --> 01:29:56.912
But they didn't stop us from listening to our conversations.

1475
01:29:56.912 --> 01:30:01.338
Every chance we got we were on the phone listening to them.

1476
01:30:01.338 --> 01:30:03.181
That was party line time.

1477
01:30:03.181 --> 01:30:10.556
We listened to a lot of calls we shouldn't have been listening to, a lot of grownup calls: Mr. [Ravielle?] . . .

1478
01:30:10.556 --> 01:30:13.138
<v TOWNER>Oh, he was on your guys' line?

1479
01:30:13.138 --> 01:30:16.456
<v AMBROSE>Oh yeah, and he was a crook of crooks.

1480
01:30:16.456 --> 01:30:20.512
Mr. [Ravielle?] lived right down the street from our house, too.

1481
01:30:20.512 --> 01:30:21.988
He had a slaughter.

1482
01:30:21.988 --> 01:30:28.625
It was only a patch of woods that separated the black Mossville community from the end of Sulphur.

1483
01:30:28.625 --> 01:30:32.681
I mean, it was just maybe a block, a city block.

1484
01:30:32.681 --> 01:30:33.788
Wouldn't you think?

1485
01:30:33.788 --> 01:30:36.000
Between the LaTour’s and us?

1486
01:30:36.000 --> 01:30:37.014
<v TOWNER>Something like that.

1487
01:30:37.014 --> 01:30:37.690
Yes. [laughs]

1488
01:30:37.690 --> 01:30:43.099
<v AMBROSE>And so we were up and down the road all the time going to the store.

1489
01:30:43.099 --> 01:30:46.818
And my uncle's other friends were the . . . what were their names?

1490
01:30:46.818 --> 01:30:51.213
They lived right on the other side . . . maybe three blocks away from us.

1491
01:30:51.213 --> 01:30:53.918
They . . . I can't think of their names now.

1492
01:30:53.918 --> 01:30:55.946
But they were a white family.

1493
01:30:55.946 --> 01:30:58.989
But they were friends of my mother's brothers, too.

1494
01:30:58.989 --> 01:31:03.384
So we didn't . . . We knew that they were white and we were black,

1495
01:31:03.384 --> 01:31:06.426
but we didn't know there was a . . . any issue.

1496
01:31:06.426 --> 01:31:10.145
And we knew that people in down South were having problems.

1497
01:31:10.145 --> 01:31:16.230
It wasn't that we were ignorant to the fact; but it was that we were just always accepted.

1498
01:31:16.230 --> 01:31:18.597
So we had to ride the bus,

1499
01:31:18.597 --> 01:31:30.429
and the only time I came in contact . . . we came in contact with racism as far as bus riding was if . . . And [Mon?] and Ms. Vincent were the only ones that ever rode the bus.

1500
01:31:30.429 --> 01:31:37.190
If a white person got on the bus, we couldn't sit up on the seat right behind the bus driver.

1501
01:31:37.190 --> 01:31:41.923
But if they didn't have anybody on the bus, we sat wherever we wanted.

1502
01:31:41.923 --> 01:31:48.009
And we would have to come to Lake Charles on the bus to do our shopping and stuff.

1503
01:31:48.009 --> 01:31:56.122
And when we came to Lake Charles we saw that there were signs that said "Coloreds" at this water fountain and whites over here,

1504
01:31:56.122 --> 01:31:59.503
but that still . . . I guess we didn't drink that much.

1505
01:31:59.503 --> 01:32:01.869
We knew about it, but it still

1506
01:32:01.869 --> 01:32:06.602
<c.p>didn't make a difference to us because we had the run of the bus.

1507
01:32:06.602 --> 01:32:10.659
It was more blacks riding the bus from over there to here.

1508
01:32:10.659 --> 01:32:16.406
And then we liked to ride the bus because we went all around English Bayou, Moss Bluff,

1509
01:32:16.406 --> 01:32:22.830
all the way down [Highway] 171 and came into Lake Charles, because it was the English Bayou bus line.

1510
01:32:22.830 --> 01:32:27.562
And then when they built the bridge, well we would just come straight across.

1511
01:32:27.562 --> 01:32:29.929
We didn't go that whole route around.

1512
01:32:29.929 --> 01:32:31.281
Do you remember that?

1513
01:32:31.281 --> 01:32:32.972
Did y'all ride the bus?

1514
01:32:32.972 --> 01:32:33.648
<v TOWNER>Not often.

1515
01:32:33.648 --> 01:32:35.000
<v AMBROSE>To Lake Charles?

1516
01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:35.762
<v TOWNER>Not often.

1517
01:32:35.762 --> 01:32:39.571
<v AMBROSE>Well that was the most integration we came up with.

1518
01:32:39.571 --> 01:32:45.667
We just knew that when it was somebody on . . . because the bus driver would tell us,

1519
01:32:45.667 --> 01:32:51.000
"If somebody white comes on the bus, y'all can't sit behind the bus driver.

1520
01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.667
You have to sit the next seat."

1521
01:32:53.667 --> 01:32:59.762
But still, I mean we knew what we were supposed to do so we did it.

1522
01:32:59.762 --> 01:33:05.476
And then we knew when we came to town we couldn't go to [Kresses?] counter.

1523
01:33:05.476 --> 01:33:08.905
We couldn't go to . . . Who else had a counter?

1524
01:33:08.905 --> 01:33:11.571
Woolworth, we couldn't go to their counter.

1525
01:33:11.571 --> 01:33:16.143
And I never shall forget the first time I went to [Muller's?].

1526
01:33:16.143 --> 01:33:29.476
I was married then and went to the . . . they had like a tea room upstairs. [Muller’s?] was one of our big department stores and it was kind of on the elite end of department stores.

1527
01:33:29.476 --> 01:33:35.571
And we knew that we couldn't go to the . . . into the store . . . into the tea room.

1528
01:33:35.571 --> 01:33:37.095
So we didn't go.

1529
01:33:37.095 --> 01:33:40.905
But I never shall forget the first time I went.

1530
01:33:40.905 --> 01:33:48.143
I felt so funny and out of sort, like I was waiting for them to tell me I wasn't

1531
01:33:48.143 --> 01:33:49.667
<c.p>supposed to be there.

1532
01:33:49.667 --> 01:33:50.429
<v TOWNER>Oh damn.

1533
01:33:50.429 --> 01:33:51.571
<v ARSENEAULT>There we go . . .

1534
01:33:51.571 --> 01:33:59.952
<v AMBROSE>And then the first time I went to McNeese [State University] and had to ride the bus from Joanne's to the school,

1535
01:33:59.952 --> 01:34:04.905
and I saw all . . . and that was in '64, the winter of '64.

1536
01:34:04.905 --> 01:34:12.143
And I saw all these people get on the bus, and during that time the bus was just packed.

1537
01:34:12.143 --> 01:34:17.095
It was like standing room only in the morning and in the evening.

1538
01:34:17.095 --> 01:34:21.667
I mean, they don't even use the buses like they do then.

1539
01:34:21.667 --> 01:34:28.524
And I remember they had these seats right around behind the bus driver that nobody was sitting in,

1540
01:34:28.524 --> 01:34:31.952
but they had all these black people standing up.

1541
01:34:31.952 --> 01:34:38.429
And I said, "There's nobody on the bus, and if you're not supposed to be sitting there,

1542
01:34:38.429 --> 01:34:43.381
well when somebody gets on, get up and just move to the back."

1543
01:34:43.381 --> 01:34:45.667
And I never could understand that.

1544
01:34:45.667 --> 01:34:55.571
I was sitting down, of course, but I often wondered what I would have done when I got on the bus and those seats were there.

1545
01:34:55.571 --> 01:35:04.333
I probably would have just sat down, because that's what we were just used to doing riding our bus, the English Bayou bus.

1546
01:35:04.333 --> 01:35:07.000
I probably would have sat down.

1547
01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:08.708
<v TOWNER>[laughs] And got thrown off.

1548
01:35:08.708 --> 01:35:09.050
<v AMBROSE>Probably.

1549
01:35:09.050 --> 01:35:16.567
I think about that, because I saw it was empty and I just couldn't imagine why these women getting on, older women.

1550
01:35:16.567 --> 01:35:19.642
I said, "Why don't they . . . the seats are empty!

1551
01:35:19.642 --> 01:35:21.692
Why don't they just sit down?

1552
01:35:21.692 --> 01:35:25.108
If somebody white got on, get up or stay sitting.

1553
01:35:25.108 --> 01:35:25.792
You was

1554
01:35:25.792 --> 01:35:26.475
<c.p>there first."

1555
01:35:26.475 --> 01:35:28.183
But I thought about that.

1556
01:35:28.183 --> 01:35:36.042
So I never got a chance to try it out because I was only there a short time having to ride the bus.

1557
01:35:36.042 --> 01:35:44.242
But I often have thought about that, if I had been on that bus and didn't have a seat, would I have sat down?

1558
01:35:44.242 --> 01:35:47.317
Because it was what I was used to doing.

1559
01:35:47.317 --> 01:35:48.000
<v TOWNER>Wow.

1560
01:35:48.000 --> 01:35:54.833
<v AMBROSE>So I guess we were naive to a lot of things, although we lived through that time.

1561
01:35:54.833 --> 01:36:02.069
We didn't experience the bad, bad parts of it like some people, which I thank God for today.

1562
01:36:02.069 --> 01:36:03.676
<v ARSENEAULT>What about Dr. King?

1563
01:36:03.676 --> 01:36:05.686
What do y'all remember about . . .?

1564
01:36:05.686 --> 01:36:07.696
<v AMBROSE>Oh God, I remember everything.

1565
01:36:07.696 --> 01:36:12.922
Everything we read . . . We read everything we could get to read on him.

1566
01:36:12.922 --> 01:36:15.735
He was on TV all the time.

1567
01:36:15.735 --> 01:36:18.951
We heard his "I Have a Dream" speech.

1568
01:36:18.951 --> 01:36:20.559
It was just mesmerizing.

1569
01:36:20.559 --> 01:36:26.990
I remember where I was when he got killed, that we heard that he had died.

1570
01:36:26.990 --> 01:36:29.000
<v TOWNER>We had an earthquake.

1571
01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:30.143
<v AMBROSE>The same day?

1572
01:36:30.143 --> 01:36:31.667
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] Yes, sure did.

1573
01:36:31.667 --> 01:36:33.190
The very same day.

1574
01:36:33.190 --> 01:36:36.619
<v AMBROSE>Oh, we were watching TV when he got killed.

1575
01:36:36.619 --> 01:36:49.190
Because we had just got through watching . . . he was talking to . . . I think it was in Memphis [Tennessee] when he was talking to this big group of people he had delivered that speech.

1576
01:36:49.190 --> 01:36:59.095
And then the very next day we were sitting down watching TV and then they came on with the thing saying that he had been shot.

1577
01:36:59.095 --> 01:37:05.190
And my God, it was just . . . it was like it was when [President] Kennedy got shot.

1578
01:37:05.190 --> 01:37:06.714
It was so unbelievable.

1579
01:37:06.714 --> 01:37:15.095
I was in chemistry class in high school and they came and say that President Kennedy had been shot in Dallas, Texas.

1580
01:37:15.095 --> 01:37:17.762
And nobody could believe . . . well, I didn't.

1581
01:37:17.762 --> 01:37:20.429
And they said that he was dead.

1582
01:37:20.429 --> 01:37:32.238
It's just like, you couldn't believe it was actually what they were saying until that night when we got home and saw it on the TV that he had actually died.

1583
01:37:32.238 --> 01:37:33.000
<v TOWNER>Wow.

1584
01:37:33.000 --> 01:37:34.189
<v AMBROSE>It was just . . .

1585
01:37:34.189 --> 01:37:37.358
<v TOWNER>And I was working at the telephone company.

1586
01:37:37.358 --> 01:37:40.528
<v AMBROSE>And they announced it over your PA system?

1587
01:37:40.528 --> 01:37:44.491
<v TOWNER>No, a lady from [Souma?] Beach came on the phone.

1588
01:37:44.491 --> 01:37:50.830
And that was when each operator had . . . you had to use plugs to talk to somebody.

1589
01:37:50.830 --> 01:37:54.396
Oh, I shouldn't have said that. [laughs] My age!

1590
01:37:54.396 --> 01:37:55.585
<v AMBROSE>[laughs] That’s right [. . .?].

1591
01:37:55.585 --> 01:38:01.132
<v TOWNER>Yes, she came on the phone and said that the president had been killed.

1592
01:38:01.132 --> 01:38:03.113
<v AMBROSE>That was an awful time.

1593
01:38:03.113 --> 01:38:11.830
I don't think . . . I think that we didn't even . . . I can't even remember what happened the rest of the day at school,

1594
01:38:11.830 --> 01:38:16.189
because everybody was just so . . . I guess nobody really believed it;

1595
01:38:16.189 --> 01:38:25.302
we just thought it was maybe something that was said, and then we were going to find out later that he was alive.

1596
01:38:25.302 --> 01:38:31.245
That whole week everybody was just kind of in nonbelief of what was going on.

1597
01:38:31.245 --> 01:38:34.811
It was the same way when they had 9/11.

1598
01:38:34.811 --> 01:38:42.340
I was on vacation. [Al?] and I were in the bed watching TV that morning, watching the Today Show.

1599
01:38:42.340 --> 01:38:49.075
And we were watching the Today Show, and you know how they show the outside scene sometimes?

1600
01:38:49.075 --> 01:38:49.472
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1601
01:38:49.472 --> 01:38:58.585
<v AMBROSE>And they were showing the Twin Towers, and all of a sudden I saw this plane and I said, "Oh my God, [Al?].”

1602
01:38:58.585 --> 01:39:02.547
I said, “That plane just hit that big, tall building."

1603
01:39:02.547 --> 01:39:06.906
I didn't know the significance of the building at the time.

1604
01:39:06.906 --> 01:39:08.887
And he said, "Oh, girl."

1605
01:39:08.887 --> 01:39:12.453
He said, "What plane going to hit a building?"

1606
01:39:12.453 --> 01:39:16.415
He said, "That building tall enough for anybody to see."

1607
01:39:16.415 --> 01:39:18.792
I said, "Al, I'm telling you."

1608
01:39:18.792 --> 01:39:25.925
And just as I said that, they came on and said that the Twin Tower had been hit.

1609
01:39:25.925 --> 01:39:31.075
And I'm looking at it and said, "You see, I told you that."

1610
01:39:31.075 --> 01:39:34.642
Then all of a sudden, the building started crumbling.

1611
01:39:34.642 --> 01:39:42.566
And whoever was taking that . . . filming that, all of a sudden, the other plane comes and hits the other one.

1612
01:39:42.566 --> 01:39:44.151
I said, "My God!"

1613
01:39:44.151 --> 01:39:47.321
I said, "Are those planes following each other?

1614
01:39:47.321 --> 01:39:54.453
They don't see that . . . I just . . ." And then, all of a sudden, they just came down like that.

1615
01:39:54.453 --> 01:40:00.792
That was so horrendous, because I thought about all the people in that . . . in those buildings.

1616
01:40:00.792 --> 01:40:04.358
I thought about all the people in those planes.

1617
01:40:04.358 --> 01:40:07.528
And then not long after, here they come

1618
01:40:07.528 --> 01:40:14.264
<c.p>with the thing about the Pentagon being hit and the plane going down in . . . where was it?

1619
01:40:14.264 --> 01:40:16.642
Maryland, or out in the woods?

1620
01:40:16.642 --> 01:40:18.623
I said, "Oh my God!

1621
01:40:18.623 --> 01:40:26.151
All this in just one day, just within a few minutes one, and a few hours the other one."

1622
01:40:26.151 --> 01:40:27.736
I couldn't believe it.

1623
01:40:27.736 --> 01:40:32.887
It was just . . . So I said, "You know, we're a part of history,

1624
01:40:32.887 --> 01:40:39.623
and with these phones and with these TVs, it makes us even more a part of history."

1625
01:40:39.623 --> 01:40:42.000
Hi Terry, how are you?

1626
01:40:42.000 --> 01:40:43.923
<v TOWNER>What time is it?

1627
01:40:43.923 --> 01:40:46.808
<v ARSENEAULT>What about medical treatment in Mossville?

1628
01:40:46.808 --> 01:40:53.538
<v AMBROSE>We had the West Calcasieu Cameron Hospital that most of the people went to.

1629
01:40:53.538 --> 01:41:00.269
Well, before they had that hospital everybody came to Lake Charles to St. Pat's.

1630
01:41:00.269 --> 01:41:02.673
That's where I was born.

1631
01:41:02.673 --> 01:41:05.558
<v TOWNER>What's the name of that clinic?

1632
01:41:05.558 --> 01:41:07.000
<v AMBROSE>Stephen's Clinic.

1633
01:41:07.000 --> 01:41:08.359
<v TOWNER>Yes, Stephen's Clinic.

1634
01:41:08.359 --> 01:41:09.266
<v AMBROSE>Dr. Stephens.

1635
01:41:09.266 --> 01:41:15.609
A lot of the people of Mossville went to Stephen's Clinic and Saint Pat's.

1636
01:41:15.609 --> 01:41:21.500
And then when they got West Cal, then people started going over there.

1637
01:41:21.500 --> 01:41:31.016
And Dr. Fellows, Charles Fellows, he lives here in Lake Charles, was he our first doctor that we had out in

1638
01:41:31.016 --> 01:41:31.469
<c.p>Mossville?

1639
01:41:31.469 --> 01:41:33.734
<v TOWNER>No. Dr. [Seals?] I believe.

1640
01:41:33.734 --> 01:41:34.641
<v AMBROSE>Dr. [Seals?]?

1641
01:41:34.641 --> 01:41:35.094
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1642
01:41:35.094 --> 01:41:36.000
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1643
01:41:36.000 --> 01:41:37.279
<v TOWNER>He was before Dr. Fellow.

1644
01:41:37.279 --> 01:41:38.814
And then we had Dr. Barrett.

1645
01:41:38.814 --> 01:41:40.093
<v AMBROSE>That was before Fellows, too?

1646
01:41:40.093 --> 01:41:40.349
<v TOWNER>No.

1647
01:41:40.349 --> 01:41:41.884
<v AMBROSE>Or that was the same time?

1648
01:41:41.884 --> 01:41:43.419
<v TOWNER>That was about the same time.

1649
01:41:43.419 --> 01:41:43.674
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1650
01:41:43.674 --> 01:41:45.209
<v TOWNER>Dr. Fellows and Dr. Barrett were . . .

1651
01:41:45.209 --> 01:41:47.000
<v AMBROSE>Well Dr. Fellows was our main . . .

1652
01:41:47.000 --> 01:41:48.000
<v TOWNER>. . . in Sulphur.

1653
01:41:48.000 --> 01:41:50.000
<v AMBROSE>Our family's main doctor.

1654
01:41:50.000 --> 01:41:55.000
Now before, like she said, Dr. [Seals?] was the older . . .

1655
01:41:55.000 --> 01:41:55.500
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1656
01:41:55.500 --> 01:41:58.000
He'd come to your house.

1657
01:41:58.000 --> 01:42:01.500
<v AMBROSE>Dr. Fellows did, too . . . made house calls.

1658
01:42:01.500 --> 01:42:03.000
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

1659
01:42:03.000 --> 01:42:03.345
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1660
01:42:03.345 --> 01:42:04.035
<v TOWNER>Oh, okay.

1661
01:42:04.035 --> 01:42:05.071
<v AMBROSE>He sure did.

1662
01:42:05.071 --> 01:42:08.867
And Al used to tell me all the time, he'd say,

1663
01:42:08.867 --> 01:42:14.389
"Dr. Fellows is going to kill all of y'all." [laughs] I said, "Well he's our doctor.

1664
01:42:14.389 --> 01:42:19.566
I don't know what to think!" [laughs] It was all we knew was Dr. Fellows.

1665
01:42:19.566 --> 01:42:21.637
But yes, he made house calls.

1666
01:42:21.637 --> 01:42:24.053
Everybody in our family went to him.

1667
01:42:24.053 --> 01:42:31.646
But I was born in St. Pat's, and my sister, the one next to me, she was born at Dr. Stephens' Clinic.

1668
01:42:31.646 --> 01:42:35.442
And then the . . . all the others were born at West Cal.

1669
01:42:35.442 --> 01:42:37.858
<v TOWNER>She asked me where was I born?

1670
01:42:37.858 --> 01:42:39.929
I hadn't even thought about that.

1671
01:42:39.929 --> 01:42:40.965
<v AMBROSE>Probably at home.

1672
01:42:40.965 --> 01:42:42.000
<v TOWNER>At home.

1673
01:42:42.000 --> 01:42:45.097
<v AMBROSE>Had a lot of home births.

1674
01:42:45.097 --> 01:42:45.613
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1675
01:42:45.613 --> 01:42:48.194
<v AMBROSE>A lot of home births.

1676
01:42:48.194 --> 01:42:51.806
<v TOWNER>Darn it, I can't remember the midwife.

1677
01:42:51.806 --> 01:42:58.000
<v AMBROSE>I don't even remember anybody talking about who midwives were back . . .

1678
01:42:58.000 --> 01:43:06.707
<v TOWNER>That big house that you was talking about, I'm thinking she had something to do with that.

1679
01:43:06.707 --> 01:43:08.244
<v AMBROSE>Oh, a midwife?

1680
01:43:08.244 --> 01:43:08.756
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1681
01:43:08.756 --> 01:43:09.268
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1682
01:43:09.268 --> 01:43:13.366
<v TOWNER>Relatives in that house, they were all relatives.

1683
01:43:13.366 --> 01:43:13.878
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1684
01:43:13.878 --> 01:43:19.000
Oh, I wish I could remember that family's name.

1685
01:43:19.000 --> 01:43:24.318
<v ARSENEAULT>So there were no doctors, there were just midwives that would give birth?

1686
01:43:24.318 --> 01:43:26.364
<v TOWNER>[Agrees] There wasn't no doctors.

1687
01:43:26.364 --> 01:43:29.227
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of equipment did they have?

1688
01:43:29.227 --> 01:43:29.636
<v TOWNER>Oh . . .

1689
01:43:29.636 --> 01:43:32.500
<v AMBROSE>Not what they have nowadays out there.

1690
01:43:32.500 --> 01:43:35.773
<v TOWNER>You ever see the . . . Do you watch Westerns?

1691
01:43:35.773 --> 01:43:37.000
That's it.

1692
01:43:37.000 --> 01:43:39.172
<v ARSENEAULT>A bowl of water and a rag.

1693
01:43:39.172 --> 01:43:43.207
<v AMBROSE>Some clean sheets, strips of sheets. [laughs] And that's probably what it was,

1694
01:43:43.207 --> 01:43:47.552
too, because they didn't have stethoscopes and monitors and all that kind of stuff.

1695
01:43:47.552 --> 01:43:47.862
<v TOWNER>No.

1696
01:43:47.862 --> 01:43:53.759
<v AMBROSE>You just had to hope that the baby had sense enough to do what he was supposed to do . . .

1697
01:43:53.759 --> 01:43:55.000
<v TOWNER>Supposed to do.

1698
01:43:55.000 --> 01:43:57.286
<v AMBROSE>. . . and the woman's body would work.

1699
01:43:57.286 --> 01:43:57.667
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1700
01:43:57.667 --> 01:44:02.619
See, that's the reason Mother had to go to the hospital with Robert.

1701
01:44:02.619 --> 01:44:06.048
He would have to be different, him and [Mitsi?].

1702
01:44:06.048 --> 01:44:09.476
<v AMBROSE>She went to the hospital for both of those?

1703
01:44:09.476 --> 01:44:09.857
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1704
01:44:09.857 --> 01:44:12.524
She had C-Sections with both of them.

1705
01:44:12.524 --> 01:44:13.286
<v AMBROSE>Oh my!

1706
01:44:13.286 --> 01:44:16.333
Well, but that was in later years, too.

1707
01:44:16.333 --> 01:44:18.238
That was in the '40s.

1708
01:44:18.238 --> 01:44:19.000
Well . . .

1709
01:44:19.000 --> 01:44:19.385
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1710
01:44:19.385 --> 01:44:21.311
<v AMBROSE>Yeah, I guess the '40s.

1711
01:44:21.311 --> 01:44:24.008
So things were a little bit different.

1712
01:44:24.008 --> 01:44:29.402
I was surprised that Momma, how they diagnosed that Momma had cancer . . . my grandmother.

1713
01:44:29.402 --> 01:44:30.557
<v TOWNER>Oh, breast cancer?

1714
01:44:30.557 --> 01:44:30.943
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1715
01:44:30.943 --> 01:44:38.262
I often wondered, because we were too young to realize that we knew that she had her breasts remove,

1716
01:44:38.262 --> 01:44:43.656
but we didn't realize how bad it was or how prevalent it would become.

1717
01:44:43.656 --> 01:44:54.057
But she had her . . . I guess she had her breasts removed with . . . I don't know if it was before Joanne was born or after Joanne was born.

1718
01:44:54.057 --> 01:44:57.525
I just remember that she had a breast removed.

1719
01:44:57.525 --> 01:45:06.000
And today . . . And I never thought to ask Momma how they found out or how she was diagnosed back in that . . .

1720
01:45:06.000 --> 01:45:07.500
<v TOWNER>With breast cancer.

1721
01:45:07.500 --> 01:45:08.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1722
01:45:08.000 --> 01:45:10.000
I just never figured.

1723
01:45:10.000 --> 01:45:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What year?

1724
01:45:11.000 --> 01:45:13.000
What year was it?

1725
01:45:13.000 --> 01:45:14.500
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

1726
01:45:14.500 --> 01:45:26.000
I know it was . . . I don't know if it was prior to my aunt being born in ‘46, or if it was after.

1727
01:45:26.000 --> 01:45:30.000
But I know it was done before 1950.

1728
01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:31.500
Yes, before 1950.

1729
01:45:31.500 --> 01:45:36.000
Did you know that she had had breast cancer?

1730
01:45:36.000 --> 01:45:37.000
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1731
01:45:37.000 --> 01:45:41.050
<v AMBROSE>So was that before you and Bubba got married?

1732
01:45:41.050 --> 01:45:41.950
<v TOWNER>Oh, yes.

1733
01:45:41.950 --> 01:45:42.400
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1734
01:45:42.400 --> 01:45:44.200
<v TOWNER>He died from a . . .

1735
01:45:44.200 --> 01:45:45.100
<v AMBROSE>[Cerebral?] hemorrhage.

1736
01:45:45.100 --> 01:45:46.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1737
01:45:46.000 --> 01:45:46.535
<v AMBROSE>Right.

1738
01:45:46.535 --> 01:45:52.419
But I just didn't know how they would have diagnosed it.

1739
01:45:52.419 --> 01:45:56.698
Or maybe she had a lump or something.

1740
01:45:56.698 --> 01:45:58.302
<v TOWNER>I don't know.

1741
01:45:58.302 --> 01:46:02.047
<v AMBROSE>So mastectomies were done even back then.

1742
01:46:02.047 --> 01:46:03.116
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

1743
01:46:03.116 --> 01:46:04.186
<v AMBROSE>Well, yeah.

1744
01:46:04.186 --> 01:46:06.326
She had a mastectomy.

1745
01:46:06.326 --> 01:46:09.000
The breast was removed.

1746
01:46:09.000 --> 01:46:10.170
<v TOWNER>Had a what?

1747
01:46:10.170 --> 01:46:12.901
<v AMBROSE>A mastectomy . . . where they remove the breasts.

1748
01:46:12.901 --> 01:46:14.851
The whole breast was removed.

1749
01:46:14.851 --> 01:46:19.922
And I just . . . I didn’t even think to ask Momma when it happened,

1750
01:46:19.922 --> 01:46:24.213
because it was a part of our . . . well I don’t know.

1751
01:46:24.213 --> 01:46:30.844
Every time I go to the doctor and I tell them my grandmother had a breast removed,

1752
01:46:30.844 --> 01:46:37.475
they don’t seem to think that that’s . . . They say it will have to be like an aunt,

1753
01:46:37.475 --> 01:46:42.546
or a first cousin, or something like that for it to affect me.

1754
01:46:42.546 --> 01:46:52.688
Because I was trying to find out because I haven’t had children, and they say you’re more prone for breast cancer if you haven’t had children.

1755
01:46:52.688 --> 01:46:53.468
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

1756
01:46:53.468 --> 01:46:55.028
<v AMBROSE>I never had . . . Yeah.

1757
01:46:55.028 --> 01:47:04.000
I don't smoke, I don't drink, but because I haven't had children then I'm prone . . . more prone than somebody who's had children.

1758
01:47:04.000 --> 01:47:06.874
<v TOWNER>Oh, I shouldn't even worry then huh?

1759
01:47:06.874 --> 01:47:08.516
<v AMBROSE>[laughs] No. You shouldn't.

1760
01:47:08.516 --> 01:47:13.442
But I was telling how the hereditary issues, because she had it.

1761
01:47:13.442 --> 01:47:19.189
And they said that I wouldn't be as prone because it was my grandmother,

1762
01:47:19.189 --> 01:47:24.526
as if it were an aunt or, I think they said, first cousin.

1763
01:47:24.526 --> 01:47:26.168
<v TOWNER>But wait a minute.

1764
01:47:26.168 --> 01:47:29.863
Now nobody in our family ever had breast cancer.

1765
01:47:29.863 --> 01:47:31.505
Lonnie had [got it?].

1766
01:47:31.505 --> 01:47:33.558
<v AMBROSE>Well that's what I said.

1767
01:47:33.558 --> 01:47:38.074
I mean, but it could be either because of something else.

1768
01:47:38.074 --> 01:47:43.000
<c.p>Maybe her daddy's side of the family, or . . . Or maybe her . . .

1769
01:47:43.000 --> 01:47:45.022
<v TOWNER>I don't know about [T?].

1770
01:47:45.022 --> 01:47:45.831
<v AMBROSE>. . . living conditions.

1771
01:47:45.831 --> 01:47:47.853
You know what I'm saying?

1772
01:47:47.853 --> 01:47:50.684
Because it can be . . . I don't know.

1773
01:47:50.684 --> 01:47:55.537
It could be anything, maybe medications or . . . There's so many other factors . . .

1774
01:47:55.537 --> 01:47:55.941
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1775
01:47:55.941 --> 01:47:57.559
<v AMBROSE>. . . that could factor in.

1776
01:47:57.559 --> 01:48:02.816
Because Al, who would have thought that he would have died with cancer?

1777
01:48:02.816 --> 01:48:06.860
Nobody in his family, that we knew of, had cancer.

1778
01:48:06.860 --> 01:48:12.118
It was always heart problems and high blood pressure, but nobody had cancer.

1779
01:48:12.118 --> 01:48:14.949
But here he is, he had cancer.

1780
01:48:14.949 --> 01:48:18.993
And there was no flag, what they call "primary condition."

1781
01:48:18.993 --> 01:48:21.419
They could never find the primary.

1782
01:48:21.419 --> 01:48:24.250
So that was one of the things.

1783
01:48:24.250 --> 01:48:27.890
But I don't know what year Momma had cancer.

1784
01:48:27.890 --> 01:48:34.765
And I don't know where she had the surgery . . . if she had the surgery at St. Pat's.

1785
01:48:34.765 --> 01:48:38.000
Had to have it at St. Pat's.

1786
01:48:38.000 --> 01:48:40.667
<v TOWNER>Yes, that was the only place.

1787
01:48:40.667 --> 01:48:42.000
<v AMBROSE>During that time.

1788
01:48:42.000 --> 01:48:42.444
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1789
01:48:42.444 --> 01:48:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about natural remedies or home remedies?

1790
01:48:46.000 --> 01:48:47.962
<v TOWNER>Oh, they believed in that.

1791
01:48:47.962 --> 01:48:49.924
<v AMBROSE>Nowadays you'd probably die [laughs].

1792
01:48:49.924 --> 01:48:53.848
And they worked, because like if somebody had nose bleeds,

1793
01:48:53.848 --> 01:49:00.519
they would put the two matches [cross?] in their head and that would stop the nose bleed.

1794
01:49:00.519 --> 01:49:08.367
Or if somebody stuck their food on a nail, they would put . . . What did they use the cob webs for?

1795
01:49:08.367 --> 01:49:10.722
<v TOWNER>They'd put that on a cut.

1796
01:49:10.722 --> 01:49:11.114
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1797
01:49:11.114 --> 01:49:17.000
<v TOWNER>You know, to help the bleeding . . . to keep it from starting to bleed again.

1798
01:49:17.000 --> 01:49:17.556
<v AMBROSE>Yes, okay.

1799
01:49:17.556 --> 01:49:20.333
Spider webs, you couldn't use any of that stuff nowadays.

1800
01:49:20.333 --> 01:49:21.167
Too much pollution.

1801
01:49:21.167 --> 01:49:23.667
<v DIGILORMO>Like a burnt match, or just a new match?

1802
01:49:23.667 --> 01:49:24.500
<v TOWNER>Just a match.

1803
01:49:24.500 --> 01:49:27.833
<v AMBROSE>I guess it was just . . . Yes, I don't know when they burned . . .

1804
01:49:27.833 --> 01:49:28.667
<v TOWNER>No it wasn't.

1805
01:49:28.667 --> 01:49:30.056
It was a burnt match.

1806
01:49:30.056 --> 01:49:31.167
It was burnt matches.

1807
01:49:31.167 --> 01:49:32.000
<v AMBROSE>Oh, okay.

1808
01:49:32.000 --> 01:49:32.226
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1809
01:49:32.226 --> 01:49:33.132
It was burnt matches.

1810
01:49:33.132 --> 01:49:34.943
<v AMBROSE>I couldn't remember if it was burnt matches.

1811
01:49:34.943 --> 01:49:36.981
I just remember them putting . . . crossing them in your . . .

1812
01:49:36.981 --> 01:49:38.792
<v TOWNER>Yes, in the top of your head. [laughs]

1813
01:49:38.792 --> 01:49:39.925
<v AMBROSE>The top of your head.

1814
01:49:39.925 --> 01:49:42.189
<v DIGILORMO>I could have used that when I was growing up.

1815
01:49:42.189 --> 01:49:44.000
I got nose bleeds all the time.

1816
01:49:44.000 --> 01:49:44.882
<v AMBROSE>Oh my.

1817
01:49:44.882 --> 01:49:46.206
Well they worked.

1818
01:49:46.206 --> 01:49:48.853
Whatever caused . . . I don't know what.

1819
01:49:48.853 --> 01:49:51.059
<v TOWNER>Don't you try that now.

1820
01:49:51.059 --> 01:49:51.500
<v AMBROSE>No.

1821
01:49:51.500 --> 01:49:58.559
<v TOWNER>And that . . . When you have a cold over your chest with that tallow and [Vick's?] [salve?].

1822
01:49:58.559 --> 01:49:59.000
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1823
01:49:59.000 --> 01:50:00.765
<v TOWNER>Tallow is beef fat.

1824
01:50:00.765 --> 01:50:11.353
And they'd cook that and get all of the grease out of it and then mix it with some [Vick's salve?] and rub you.

1825
01:50:11.353 --> 01:50:14.000
Boy, you'd smell for days.

1826
01:50:14.000 --> 01:50:15.615
<v DIGILORMO>I bet so.

1827
01:50:15.615 --> 01:50:18.846
<v TOWNER>And when you had the mumps . . .

1828
01:50:18.846 --> 01:50:19.385
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1829
01:50:19.385 --> 01:50:19.923
<v TOWNER>. . . sardines.

1830
01:50:19.923 --> 01:50:21.000
<v AMBROSE>Sardines.

1831
01:50:21.000 --> 01:50:22.833
<v TOWNER>And rub your throat with it.

1832
01:50:22.833 --> 01:50:24.667
<v AMBROSE>The oil off of the sardines.

1833
01:50:24.667 --> 01:50:26.500
And then you'd stink for days.

1834
01:50:26.500 --> 01:50:28.333
<v TOWNER>You'd smell for a week. [laughs]

1835
01:50:28.333 --> 01:50:29.556
<v AMBROSE>You'd smell for days.

1836
01:50:29.556 --> 01:50:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it didn't work unless you'd smell?

1837
01:50:32.000 --> 01:50:32.975
<v AMBROSE>That's exactly right.

1838
01:50:32.975 --> 01:50:35.575
Maybe that was the smell that did it.

1839
01:50:35.575 --> 01:50:38.825
And I remember when little bitty babies had . . . were teething.

1840
01:50:38.825 --> 01:50:44.025
There was some type of root that they would go out in the woods and get.

1841
01:50:44.025 --> 01:50:48.575
They would string it on a string and tie it around the baby's neck.

1842
01:50:48.575 --> 01:50:50.525
I don't know what that was.

1843
01:50:50.525 --> 01:50:53.450
<v TOWNER>That was to keep them from having a fever.

1844
01:50:53.450 --> 01:50:53.775
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1845
01:50:53.775 --> 01:50:54.100
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1846
01:50:54.100 --> 01:50:57.350
<v AMBROSE>And then they would put silver dimes on their necks . . .

1847
01:50:57.350 --> 01:50:58.000
<v TOWNER>Yes.

1848
01:50:58.000 --> 01:51:00.057
<v AMBROSE>. . . when they were teething.

1849
01:51:00.057 --> 01:51:02.114
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the root?

1850
01:51:02.114 --> 01:51:03.657
<v AMBROSE>Was it sassafras?

1851
01:51:03.657 --> 01:51:07.771
What did they use the mamou tea for?

1852
01:51:07.771 --> 01:51:09.829
<v TOWNER>That was for tea.

1853
01:51:09.829 --> 01:51:13.429
<v AMBROSE>What would they use the tea for?

1854
01:51:13.429 --> 01:51:16.000
<v TOWNER>Oh, just to drink.

1855
01:51:16.000 --> 01:51:19.034
<v AMBROSE>Oh, I thought it was for some illness.

1856
01:51:19.034 --> 01:51:23.207
<v TOWNER>Put some alcohol in it and drink it as a toddy.

1857
01:51:23.207 --> 01:51:23.966
<v AMBROSE>Okay. [laughs]

1858
01:51:23.966 --> 01:51:25.862
<v DIGILORMO>It would cure your throat?

1859
01:51:25.862 --> 01:51:26.241
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1860
01:51:26.241 --> 01:51:27.000
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees]

1861
01:51:27.000 --> 01:51:28.823
<v TOWNER>That cured a lot of throats.

1862
01:51:28.823 --> 01:51:30.342
<v AMBROSE>I know they made it.

1863
01:51:30.342 --> 01:51:33.380
I couldn't remember . . . and I know there was a sassafras.

1864
01:51:33.380 --> 01:51:36.114
<v TOWNER>I don't know what kind of root that was.

1865
01:51:36.114 --> 01:51:36.722
<v AMBROSE>The mamou?

1866
01:51:36.722 --> 01:51:39.456
<v TOWNER>I don't know why I keep fiddling with this.

1867
01:51:39.456 --> 01:51:44.316
I don't know what kind of root that was that they put on the babies' necks.

1868
01:51:44.316 --> 01:51:51.000
<v AMBROSE>But I know it was a root and it was like jointed, and they would put that on their little necks.

1869
01:51:51.000 --> 01:51:57.000
<v TOWNER>Yes, and if they had a fever, it eventually would dry up.

1870
01:51:57.000 --> 01:52:02.500
And you'd take it off and put a fresh one on.

1871
01:52:02.500 --> 01:52:07.500
Kids never had a bad fever, never a high fever.

1872
01:52:07.500 --> 01:52:10.000
<v AMBROSE>Well they had mamou tea.

1873
01:52:10.000 --> 01:52:11.500
They had sassafras.

1874
01:52:11.500 --> 01:52:17.500
And I think sassafras and . . . isn't sassafras and sage the same thing?

1875
01:52:17.500 --> 01:52:19.500
Or is it different?

1876
01:52:19.500 --> 01:52:20.500
<v TOWNER>They're different.

1877
01:52:20.500 --> 01:52:21.000
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

1878
01:52:21.000 --> 01:52:23.000
Well then it's sassafras.

1879
01:52:23.000 --> 01:52:28.500
They had sassafras tea and . . . what other one did they have?

1880
01:52:28.500 --> 01:52:30.000
<v TOWNER>Pine top.

1881
01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:31.086
<v AMBROSE>What is that?

1882
01:52:31.086 --> 01:52:32.172
<v TOWNER>Pine top tea.

1883
01:52:32.172 --> 01:52:33.983
You know what pine is?

1884
01:52:33.983 --> 01:52:35.069
Pine . . . the leaves . . .

1885
01:52:35.069 --> 01:52:35.793
<v AMBROSE>The tree?

1886
01:52:35.793 --> 01:52:37.241
<v TOWNER>. . . of a pine tree.

1887
01:52:37.241 --> 01:52:37.603
Yes.

1888
01:52:37.603 --> 01:52:40.138
You never had a tea like that?

1889
01:52:40.138 --> 01:52:40.500
<v AMBROSE>[Disagrees]

1890
01:52:40.500 --> 01:52:41.224
<v TOWNER>Oh, shit.

1891
01:52:41.224 --> 01:52:44.121
I used to love to get a cold.

1892
01:52:44.121 --> 01:52:45.569
Daddy would make it.

1893
01:52:45.569 --> 01:52:48.466
<v AMBROSE>And put a little stuff in it? [laughs]

1894
01:52:48.466 --> 01:52:48.828
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1895
01:52:48.828 --> 01:52:51.000
<v DIGILORMO>Was it a pine cone?

1896
01:52:51.000 --> 01:52:51.706
<v TOWNER>No, the straw.

1897
01:52:51.706 --> 01:52:52.176
<v AMBROSE>The straw.

1898
01:52:52.176 --> 01:52:52.882
<v DIGILORMO>Oh, the straw.

1899
01:52:52.882 --> 01:52:53.118
Okay.

1900
01:52:53.118 --> 01:52:53.824
<v AMBROSE>The green straw.

1901
01:52:53.824 --> 01:52:55.000
<v TOWNER>Yes, the green straw.

1902
01:52:55.000 --> 01:52:59.245
<v DIGILORMO>When you said "pine top" I was thinking like the top of one of those . . .

1903
01:52:59.245 --> 01:53:00.094
<v TOWNER>No, no. [laughs].

1904
01:53:00.094 --> 01:53:00.943
This was the . . .

1905
01:53:00.943 --> 01:53:03.491
<v AMBROSE>Now I know that they used to take the . . .

1906
01:53:03.491 --> 01:53:04.057
<v TOWNER>. . . green stuff.

1907
01:53:04.057 --> 01:53:07.170
<v AMBROSE>. . . sap that came out of the pine tree and chew it.

1908
01:53:07.170 --> 01:53:09.151
But I don't know if that was . . .

1909
01:53:09.151 --> 01:53:10.000
<v TOWNER>For what?

1910
01:53:10.000 --> 01:53:16.481
<v AMBROSE>I don't know what it's for I just remember them saying that you could chew that sticky stuff that came out,

1911
01:53:16.481 --> 01:53:18.642
the sap, out of the pine tree.

1912
01:53:18.642 --> 01:53:19.877
They would chew it.

1913
01:53:19.877 --> 01:53:24.506
Now I don't know if that was for like a tooth ache medicine or what.

1914
01:53:24.506 --> 01:53:27.901
But that was . . . some of the old people would say that.

1915
01:53:27.901 --> 01:53:28.519
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

1916
01:53:28.519 --> 01:53:32.222
<v AMBROSE>That they used to chew that sap out of the pine trees.

1917
01:53:32.222 --> 01:53:33.765
<v TOWNER>Girl, ain't no telling. [laughs]

1918
01:53:33.765 --> 01:53:35.000
<v AMBROSE>I don't know.

1919
01:53:35.000 --> 01:53:36.259
<v TOWNER>It's no telling.

1920
01:53:36.259 --> 01:53:40.036
<v AMBROSE>I know that was one of the other things.

1921
01:53:40.036 --> 01:53:43.393
We didn't have too many remedies like that.

1922
01:53:43.393 --> 01:53:49.688
The only ones I really remember is if you stuck a nail in your foot,

1923
01:53:49.688 --> 01:53:59.759
they would take and put the cob webs and fat, like the salted pork, they would put that on there and tie it up.

1924
01:53:59.759 --> 01:54:08.152
And that would draw the . . . I guess draw the poison or the inflammation out so your foot wouldn't get infected.

1925
01:54:08.152 --> 01:54:09.830
And I know matches.

1926
01:54:09.830 --> 01:54:11.089
I remember that.

1927
01:54:11.089 --> 01:54:15.286
And it was something they did with a paper bag.

1928
01:54:15.286 --> 01:54:16.125
<v TOWNER>For hiccups?

1929
01:54:16.125 --> 01:54:17.384
<v AMBROSE>Was it hiccups?

1930
01:54:17.384 --> 01:54:21.161
Tell you to breathe in the paper bag and . . .

1931
01:54:21.161 --> 01:54:22.000
<v TOWNER>[Agrees]

1932
01:54:22.000 --> 01:54:22.955
<v AMBROSE>Okay, yes.

1933
01:54:22.955 --> 01:54:25.343
That might have been hiccups.

1934
01:54:25.343 --> 01:54:33.940
And I know when we had the mumps we had all this sardine oil on our jaws. [laughs]

1935
01:54:33.940 --> 01:54:44.925
<v TOWNER>What if you go to a kid tell them . . . with the mumps and tell them you want to rub on that sardine oil.

1936
01:54:44.925 --> 01:54:45.881
<v AMBROSE>Oh, gosh.

1937
01:54:45.881 --> 01:54:47.313
<v TOWNER>Oh my goodness.

1938
01:54:47.313 --> 01:54:52.567
<v AMBROSE>And I think about how stinky your bed must have been.

1939
01:54:52.567 --> 01:54:54.000
<v TOWNER>Oh! [laughs]

1940
01:54:54.000 --> 01:54:55.323
<v ARSENEAULT>What about chicken pox?

1941
01:54:55.323 --> 01:54:55.984
<v AMBROSE>Calamine lotion.

1942
01:54:55.984 --> 01:54:59.952
That was the only thing I knew that they used on us.

1943
01:54:59.952 --> 01:55:02.927
<v TOWNER>I don't know what they did for chicken pox.

1944
01:55:02.927 --> 01:55:04.250
<v AMBROSE>We had calamine lotion.

1945
01:55:04.250 --> 01:55:07.226
That was a God-send when I was growing up.

1946
01:55:07.226 --> 01:55:12.185
Calamine lotion was used for everything— every little pimple, every little red bug, every speck.

1947
01:55:12.185 --> 01:55:19.790
And they used to . . . When we were kids during the summer months we would be outside playing, we got these little red bugs.

1948
01:55:19.790 --> 01:55:23.097
I don't know if they even have red bugs nowadays.

1949
01:55:23.097 --> 01:55:25.742
They probably killed them all with the pollution.

1950
01:55:25.742 --> 01:55:26.734
I don't know.

1951
01:55:26.734 --> 01:55:31.363
But they would get in your skin and they would make a little whelp,

1952
01:55:31.363 --> 01:55:35.000
and you could actually see them . . . You've seen them before?

1953
01:55:35.000 --> 01:55:38.429
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees] When I go camping I get them.

1954
01:55:38.429 --> 01:55:41.429
<v AMBROSE>And we'd have to bathe in soda.

1955
01:55:41.429 --> 01:55:43.571
Put soda in the water.

1956
01:55:43.571 --> 01:55:44.429
<v ARSENEAULT>Baking soda?

1957
01:55:44.429 --> 01:55:47.429
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] Baking soda in our bath water.

1958
01:55:47.429 --> 01:55:49.143
<v ARSENEAULT>And it killed them?

1959
01:55:49.143 --> 01:55:50.000
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees]

1960
01:55:50.000 --> 01:55:52.571
<v TOWNER>Now when you go camping you know.

1961
01:55:52.571 --> 01:55:52.939
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1962
01:55:52.939 --> 01:55:55.143
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] That's what we bathed in.

1963
01:55:55.143 --> 01:55:57.347
Probably got a different strain now.

1964
01:55:57.347 --> 01:56:01.020
You'll probably have to use something stronger than baking soda.

1965
01:56:01.020 --> 01:56:03.959
But I remember Mom always kept baking soda,

1966
01:56:03.959 --> 01:56:11.306
because we'd get red bugs out there playing in the grass and we'd have to take baths in baking soda.

1967
01:56:11.306 --> 01:56:12.776
<v ARSENEAULT>What about mosquito bites?

1968
01:56:12.776 --> 01:56:13.878
<v AMBROSE>We would smoke.

1969
01:56:13.878 --> 01:56:17.551
We were outside a lot when I was growing up.

1970
01:56:17.551 --> 01:56:17.751
They would always keep old wool rags and they would burn it to make a smoke, and that kept the mosquitos

1971
01:56:17.551 --> 01:56:25.265
<c.p>. . .

1972
01:56:25.265 --> 01:56:26.000
<v TOWNER>Away.

1973
01:56:26.000 --> 01:56:27.115
<v AMBROSE>. . . away from us.

1974
01:56:27.115 --> 01:56:30.088
<v TOWNER>Sometimes they'd put sulfur in them, didn't they?

1975
01:56:30.088 --> 01:56:30.460
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

1976
01:56:30.460 --> 01:56:31.575
Sometimes they would.

1977
01:56:31.575 --> 01:56:33.062
They would use sulfur.

1978
01:56:33.062 --> 01:56:36.779
But that seemed to have kept them away pretty good.

1979
01:56:36.779 --> 01:56:43.841
Now you go and buy all this highfalutin lights and things, and the ones I always bought never worked.

1980
01:56:43.841 --> 01:56:49.788
We still had bites, because I remember when we had our gazebo built I bought two.

1981
01:56:49.788 --> 01:56:52.761
I don't know why, because I'm never outside.

1982
01:56:52.761 --> 01:56:58.336
I wanted to go sit in the gazebo, but I had . . . [laughs] I had overkill.

1983
01:56:58.336 --> 01:57:01.310
And Al said, "Why aren't you buying these?"

1984
01:57:01.310 --> 01:57:03.912
And he said, "Because you're never outside."

1985
01:57:03.912 --> 01:57:08.000
I said, "Well, just in case I am, I'm ready."

1986
01:57:08.000 --> 01:57:10.276
<v TOWNER>You'll be ready.

1987
01:57:10.276 --> 01:57:13.310
<v ARSENEAULT>What about stomach aches?

1988
01:57:13.310 --> 01:57:14.069
<v TOWNER>Soda . . .

1989
01:57:14.069 --> 01:57:18.621
<v AMBROSE>It was Pepto-Bismol during my time.

1990
01:57:18.621 --> 01:57:20.897
<v TOWNER>. . . and warm water.

1991
01:57:20.897 --> 01:57:26.966
I had a teaspoon of soda and a . . .

1992
01:57:26.966 --> 01:57:30.000
<v AMBROSE>Glass of water.

1993
01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:32.061
<v TOWNER>. . . glass of warm water.

1994
01:57:32.061 --> 01:57:35.152
And that would be about it.

1995
01:57:35.152 --> 01:57:36.697
<v AMBROSE>We had Pepto-Bismol.

1996
01:57:36.697 --> 01:57:37.727
<v TOWNER>And nasty.

1997
01:57:37.727 --> 01:57:39.273
<v AMBROSE>We had Pepto-Bismol.

1998
01:57:39.273 --> 01:57:41.848
<v TOWNER>Oh they had no Pepto-Bismol.

1999
01:57:41.848 --> 01:57:42.879
<v AMBROSE>We did!

2000
01:57:42.879 --> 01:57:47.000
<v TOWNER>Well maybe when you guys were kids.

2001
01:57:47.000 --> 01:57:48.098
<v AMBROSE>Well, yeah!

2002
01:57:48.098 --> 01:57:50.843
I don't remember no soda.

2003
01:57:50.843 --> 01:57:56.333
<v TOWNER>[laughs] Soda, yes, soda's really good for an upset stomach.

2004
01:57:56.333 --> 01:58:01.275
<v AMBROSE>I'm trying to think what else we had medicine-wise.

2005
01:58:01.275 --> 01:58:02.922
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything for headaches?

2006
01:58:02.922 --> 01:58:05.667
<v TOWNER>[Disagrees] I don't remember nothing . . .

2007
01:58:05.667 --> 01:58:13.902
<v AMBROSE>Now one of the old things—we never did it, but I heard it—was palmetto leaves.

2008
01:58:13.902 --> 01:58:15.000
<v TOWNER>Who?

2009
01:58:15.000 --> 01:58:15.443
<v AMBROSE>Palmetto.

2010
01:58:15.443 --> 01:58:21.639
Remember, it's those plants that grew like going through where grandma was . . . grandma lived.

2011
01:58:21.639 --> 01:58:23.410
They like fanned out.

2012
01:58:23.410 --> 01:58:26.951
That, I heard from my mother in law.

2013
01:58:26.951 --> 01:58:29.607
I didn't hear that from Momma.

2014
01:58:29.607 --> 01:58:33.148
But whenever she'd get these really bad headaches,

2015
01:58:33.148 --> 01:58:40.230
she would take it and put that . . . [Is that?] Daddy trying to get in the driveway.

2016
01:58:40.230 --> 01:58:46.869
They would put those . . . that palmetto leaf on her forehead and then tie it around.

2017
01:58:46.869 --> 01:58:50.852
And she always kept them growing in her yard.

2018
01:58:50.852 --> 01:58:51.738
<v TOWNER>Oh really?

2019
01:58:51.738 --> 01:58:52.180
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

2020
01:58:52.180 --> 01:58:57.049
It's kind of a . . . In fact, they have a Palmetto, Louisiana.

2021
01:58:57.049 --> 01:59:05.902
From what I understand they have a lot of those. . . or they used to have a lot of those plants.

2022
01:59:05.902 --> 01:59:11.656
And they grow like in . . . I know going through the woods to Grandma's,

2023
01:59:11.656 --> 01:59:18.295
in that boggy swampy like place in there, they'd have those palmettos growing in there.

2024
01:59:18.295 --> 01:59:21.836
I don't know if they'd have them anymore.

2025
01:59:21.836 --> 01:59:32.016
But that's the only thing that I remember, because she would have those headaches and that's the only thing I remember her using.

2026
01:59:32.016 --> 01:59:36.000
But I don't remember what . . . other than Aspirin.

2027
01:59:36.000 --> 01:59:41.634
<v TOWNER>I don't remember anything here, like I say, except Aspirin.

2028
01:59:41.634 --> 01:59:45.577
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they make the pine tea?

2029
01:59:45.577 --> 01:59:53.465
<v TOWNER>Water and the . . . what do you call those things, the green stuff on them?

2030
01:59:53.465 --> 01:59:54.592
<v AMBROSE>Pine needles.

2031
01:59:54.592 --> 01:59:55.718
<v TOWNER>Pine needles.

2032
01:59:55.718 --> 02:00:16.000
You put—well, it's according to how much you make—in a pot and boil it for maybe about ten minutes and then let it sit and just pour a cup . . . pour you a cup of it.

2033
02:00:16.000 --> 02:00:18.769
<v AMBROSE>Did you put sugar in it?

2034
02:00:18.769 --> 02:00:19.231
<v TOWNER>Yes.

2035
02:00:19.231 --> 02:00:26.154
You had to put sugar in it and . . . what else did Daddy put in it?

2036
02:00:26.154 --> 02:00:27.538
A little lemon.

2037
02:00:27.538 --> 02:00:29.385
Yes, lemon and sugar.

2038
02:00:29.385 --> 02:00:35.846
It seemed like . . . Oh, it was so good. [laughs] I wouldn't drink it now.

2039
02:00:35.846 --> 02:00:37.692
<v AMBROSE>No. Too much pollution.

2040
02:00:37.692 --> 02:00:38.154
<v TOWNER>Yes.

2041
02:00:38.154 --> 02:00:42.769
<v AMBROSE>Now I know one of the things that [Ree?] does.

2042
02:00:42.769 --> 02:00:47.846
When we have like a cold, she makes us a toddy.

2043
02:00:47.846 --> 02:00:52.000
Aunt [Ree?], what you put in our toddy?

2044
02:00:52.000 --> 02:00:55.830
<v TOWNER>It makes a cough syrup too.

2045
02:00:55.830 --> 02:00:58.383
<v AMBROSE>Yes, a cough syrup.

2046
02:00:58.383 --> 02:01:00.936
That's what it is.

2047
02:01:00.936 --> 02:01:04.128
<v TOWNER>Honey, [E&amp;J?], and peppermint candy.

2048
02:01:04.128 --> 02:01:07.957
<v AMBROSE>That makes the best cough syrup.

2049
02:01:07.957 --> 02:01:09.872
I don't know . . .

2050
02:01:09.872 --> 02:01:11.149
<v TOWNER>Cure anything.

2051
02:01:11.149 --> 02:01:18.170
<v AMBROSE>. . . which does it. [laughs] I don't know which does it, but . . .

2052
02:01:18.170 --> 02:01:19.447
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds good.

2053
02:01:19.447 --> 02:01:22.000
Sounds very festive.

2054
02:01:22.000 --> 02:01:24.294
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees] In fact, I have some from last year.

2055
02:01:24.294 --> 02:01:26.333
I put it in the bottom, in my . . .

2056
02:01:26.333 --> 02:01:27.608
<v TOWNER>I got to make some.

2057
02:01:27.608 --> 02:01:27.863
<v AMBROSE>. . . crisper.

2058
02:01:27.863 --> 02:01:29.902
I don't know if I can use it.

2059
02:01:29.902 --> 02:01:33.216
It might not be any good since the last time I had it.

2060
02:01:33.216 --> 02:01:33.471
<v TOWNER>Yes.

2061
02:01:33.471 --> 02:01:35.000
<v AMBROSE>Oh, it don't go bad?

2062
02:01:35.000 --> 02:01:36.907
<v TOWNER>No. Alcohol doesn't go bad.

2063
02:01:36.907 --> 02:01:39.958
<v AMBROSE>Well it's in the bottom of my crisper.

2064
02:01:39.958 --> 02:01:41.483
I had some left.

2065
02:01:41.483 --> 02:01:43.771
<v TOWNER>Yes, I got to make some.

2066
02:01:43.771 --> 02:01:54.449
One of these days [have them call us?] to see if I got any made. [Desi?] got sick with a cold and she came down here one time.

2067
02:01:54.449 --> 02:01:58.263
She didn't do any driving. [Reynard?] did all the driving.

2068
02:01:58.263 --> 02:02:04.364
She started drinking it when she left the house and drank that all the way home.

2069
02:02:04.364 --> 02:02:07.797
She said she slept good on the way home.

2070
02:02:07.797 --> 02:02:09.703
<v AMBROSE>[laughs] I know she did!

2071
02:02:09.703 --> 02:02:12.373
I know she was good and full.

2072
02:02:12.373 --> 02:02:13.517
Felt really good.

2073
02:02:13.517 --> 02:02:14.280
That's funny.

2074
02:02:14.280 --> 02:02:16.568
Well y'all, I've got to go.

2075
02:02:16.568 --> 02:02:20.000
I've got to go see about my dress.

2076
02:02:20.000 --> 02:02:22.053
<v ARSENEAULT>I think we're at two hours.

2077
02:02:22.053 --> 02:02:24.789
This is a good place to stop. [laughs]

2078
02:02:24.789 --> 02:02:25.474
<v TOWNER>[laughs] Yes.

2079
02:02:25.474 --> 02:02:27.868
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you believe you talked that long?

2080
02:02:27.868 --> 02:02:29.579
It feels like ten minutes.

2081
02:02:29.579 --> 02:02:30.947
<v TOWNER>It's been two hours?

2082
02:02:30.947 --> 02:02:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Two hours and six minutes.

2083
02:02:33.000 --> 02:02:37.878
<v TOWNER>And when your girl got here, I told her I didn't think I had nothing to say for no hour.

2084
02:02:37.878 --> 02:02:39.585
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you so much for sharing.

2085
02:02:39.585 --> 02:02:40.317
<v TOWNER>Oh, no problem.

2086
02:02:40.317 --> 02:02:43.000
I'm going to see if I can find any old . . .

2087
02:02:43.000 --> 02:02:43.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4546.

2088
02:02:43.429 --> 02:02:43.857
End Session I.]
